(window.LH_SEARCH = window.LH_SEARCH || {})["mlops"] = {"s":"mlops","t":[["mlops-setup","Set up your computer for machine learning","1.1"],["mlops-lifecycle","The MLOps lifecycle and reproducible projects","1.2"],["mlops-pipelines","Pipelines and saving models","2.1"],["mlops-serving","Serving models with Flask, FastAPI and Django","2.2"],["mlops-docker","Docker and CI/CD for models","2.3"],["mlops-tracking","Experiment tracking and the model registry","3.1"],["mlops-monitoring","Monitoring and drift","3.2"],["mlops-explain-fair","Explainability and fairness","3.3"],["mlops-system-design","Designing an ML system end to end","4.1"],["mlops-revision","MLOps revision and certification practice","4.2"],["cheatsheet","Cheatsheet","📄"]],"c":[[0,"","Set up your computer for machine learning","Python, virtual environments, Git, VS Code, Docker and GPUs set up on macOS, Windows, Linux and ChromeOS. · setup · install Python · virtual environment · pip · uv · requirements.txt · Docker · WSL 2 · ChromeOS Linux · GPU Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 1: Set up and plan›Setup on every system Step 1 · Topic 1 of 2 · Beginner Install Python, a terminal, virtual environments, pip and uv, Git, VS Code, Docker and GPU support, with separate steps for macOS, Windows, Linux and ChromeOS, and fix the problems people hit most. About 50 min9 lessons2 animations10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What you need and why 2Install Python and open a terminal 3Virtual environments and packages 4Git, VS Code and Jupyter 5Docker on each system 6GPUs, the cloud and common problems 7venv, uv, conda and Docker compared 8From beginner to expert 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[0,"tools","What you need and why","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: The Python Packaging User Guide, the Python documentation on venv, and the setup guides of Git, Visual Studio Code and Docker. How the terminal finds pythonWhen you type a command, the terminal looks through the folders listed in the PATH variable, in order, and runs the first program with that name. Activating a virtual environment simply puts its folder at the front of PATH. Step 0 of 5 System Virtual environment Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this macOS or Linux without a virtual environment: many systems have only python3, so typing python finds nothing. Activate the environment: its folder is checked first, so python and pip now mean the project’s own copies. What it is A machine learning setup has six parts: Python itself, a terminal to type commands, a virtual environment per project, a package installer (pip or uv), Git to track versions, and an editor such as VS Code. For serving and deployment you add Docker, and for big models a GPU or a cloud notebook. Why we need it Every later chapter of this subject runs these tools. The same steps also mean that a model behaves the same on your laptop, a teammate’s laptop and a server, which is the first promise of MLOps. How it works Install Python, open a terminal, create one virtual environment for each project, install packages into it, and save the exact versions in requirements.txt. Then add Git, an editor and Docker. The lessons below give the steps for macOS, Windows, Linux and ChromeOS. Open only the panel for your system. Tool What it is Why ML needs it Python 3.12 or newer The language and its interpreter NumPy, pandas, scikit-learn and PyTorch need a recent Python Terminal A window where you type commands Installing, running scripts, Git, Docker Virtual environment A folder with its own Python and packages Each project keeps its own versions pip or uv Package installers Install libraries from PyPI, the Python package index Git Version control History of code and configs, teamwork, CI VS Code Editor with Python and Jupyter support Write, run and debug code and notebooks Docker Runs apps in containers Ship the model with everything it needs GPU or cloud notebook Fast parallel hardware Training deep networks in hours, not days Python # Check that your computer is ready for the MLOps chapters.# Run it inside your activated virtual environment: python check_setup.pyimport platformimport sysfrom importlib.metadata importPackageNotFoundError, versionprint('Python ', platform.python_version(), '(needs 3.12 or newer)', 'OK'if sys.version_info >= (3, 12) else'TOO OLD')print('System ', platform.system(), platform.machine())print('Inside a virtual environment:', sys.prefix != sys.base_prefix)packages = ['numpy', 'pandas', 'scikit-learn', 'scipy', 'joblib', 'flask', 'fastapi', 'uvicorn', 'django', 'pydantic']missing = []for name in packages:try:print(f' {name:13s} {version(name)}')exceptPackageNotFoundError:print(f' {name:13s} not installed') missing.append(name)if missing:print('Install the missing ones with: pip install ' + ' '.join(missing))else:print('Everything is installed.') Output Python 3.14.6 (needs 3.12 or newer) OK System Linux x86_64 Inside a virtual environment: True numpy 2.4.6 pandas 3.0.2 scikit-learn 1.8.0 scipy 1.18.0 joblib 1.6.0 flask 3.1.3 fastapi 0.162.6 uvicorn 0.54.0 django 6.1.3 pydantic 2.14.3 Everything is installed. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 check_setup.py. Remember Run this check after each part of the setup. The output above is from the computer that built this page: Python 3.14.6 on Linux inside a virtual environment, with every package installed. Inside this web page, the same program runs in the browser’s own Python, so it reports a different system and that the web packages are not installed. Lesson 2 of 9"],[0,"python","Install Python and open a terminal","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Python documentation: Using Python on Windows, Using Python on macOS, and Using Python on Unix platforms, plus PEP 668 and Google’s help page Set up Linux on your Chromebook. What it is A terminal (also called a shell or command line) is a text window for running programs. Python is installed once per computer, and you can have several versions side by side. The PATH setting lists the folders the terminal searches when you type a command. Why we need it Most problems beginners hit are not about ML at all: the wrong Python runs, or the terminal cannot find it. Knowing where Python is installed and how PATH works saves hours. How it works Pick your system below and follow the steps. Finish with python3 --version (or python --version on Windows) and check that it says 3.12 or newer. Install Python 3.14 macOS Open Terminal (press Command and Space, type Terminal, press Return). Install Apple’s command line tools. They include Git and the compilers some packages need. A window asks you to confirm. Install Python 3.14 with the official installer from python.org/downloads (a .pkg file that works on Apple silicon and Intel), or with Homebrew if you use it. On macOS the command is python3. A plain python usually does not exist until you activate a virtual environment. Terminal (macOS)shell xcode-select --install # optional, with Homebrew (brew.sh) instead of the python.org installer brew install python@3.14 python3 --version # Python 3.14.x uname -m # arm64 = Apple silicon, x86_64 = Intel Windows 10 and 11 Open PowerShell (press the Windows key, type PowerShell, press Enter). Windows Terminal is a good home for it. Install Python 3.14 from python.org/downloads, or with winget, which ships with Windows 10 and 11. In the python.org installer, tick Add python.exe to PATH. Close and reopen PowerShell so it sees the new PATH, then check the version. The py launcher also works and can pick between installed versions. If typing python opens the Microsoft Store, turn off the python.exe and python3.exe entries in Settings, Apps, Advanced app settings, App execution aliases. PowerShell (Windows)shell winget install Python.Python.3.14 python --version # Python 3.14.x py --list # every Python the launcher can find Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) Open a terminal (Ctrl, Alt and T on Ubuntu). Most distributions already have Python 3. Install the venv module, pip and Git from the package manager. Ubuntu 24.04 ships Python 3.12, which is new enough. Do not use sudo pip install. Debian, Ubuntu and Fedora mark the system Python as externally managed (PEP 668), and pip refuses to change it outside a virtual environment. For a newer Python than your distribution offers, install uv (next lesson) and run uv python install 3.14. Terminal (Linux)shell # Ubuntu and Debian sudo apt update sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip git # Fedora sudo dnf install -y python3 python3-pip git python3 --version ChromeOS (Chromebook) Open Settings, choose About ChromeOS, then Developers, and turn on the Linux development environment. Give it at least 20 GB of disk if you can. A Terminal app appears. The Linux part is a Debian container, so every Linux command on this site works in it. Its Python may be older than 3.12, so install a newer one with uv. Files you want to use in Linux go in the Linux files folder of the Files app. School or work Chromebooks may have Linux turned off by the administrator. Then use Google Colab or Kaggle notebooks in the browser, and the in page Python of this site. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell sudo apt update sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip git curl curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh # installs uv uv python install 3.14 python3 --version In the exam Two names, one idea: macOS, Linux and ChromeOS call it python3, Windows calls it python or py. Inside an activated virtual environment, python works everywhere. Lesson 3 of 9"],[0,"venv","Virtual environments and packages","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Python documentation, venv module, the Python Packaging User Guide, Installing packages using pip and virtual environments, and the uv documentation. What it is A virtual environment is a folder, usually called .venv, that holds its own copy of the Python program and its own site-packages folder for libraries. Activating it puts its folder at the front of PATH. pip installs packages from PyPI. uv is a newer, much faster tool that does the work of pip and venv and can also install Python itself. requirements.txt lists the exact versions. Why we need it Different projects need different versions: an old model may need NumPy 1.26 while a new one needs NumPy 2.4. One shared install can hold only one version, so installing one project breaks another. With an environment per project and pinned versions, anyone can rebuild the same set of packages, which is what makes results reproducible. How it works Create the environment inside the project folder, activate it every time you work on the project, install packages, and save them with pip freeze. Never commit the .venv folder to Git, commit requirements.txt instead. Create a project environment macOS macOS, Linux and ChromeOS use the same commands. The environment folder is .venv and the activate script is in .venv/bin. Terminal (macOS)shell mkdir ml-project && cd ml-project python3 -m venv .venv source .venv/bin/activate # the prompt now starts with (.venv) python -m pip install --upgrade pip pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn joblib pip freeze > requirements.txt deactivate # leave the environment Windows 10 and 11 Windows keeps the scripts in .venv\\Scripts. PowerShell blocks scripts by default, so allow local ones once for your account. PowerShell (Windows)shell mkdir ml-project; cd ml-project py -3.14 -m venv .venv Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned # once, answer Y .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 # in Command Prompt: .venv\\Scripts\\activate.bat python -m pip install --upgrade pip pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn joblib pip freeze > requirements.txt deactivate Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) The same as macOS. If python3 -m venv says ensurepip is not available, install the python3-venv package first. Terminal (Linux)shell mkdir ml-project && cd ml-project python3 -m venv .venv source .venv/bin/activate python -m pip install --upgrade pip pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn joblib pip freeze > requirements.txt ChromeOS (Chromebook) Inside the Linux terminal the commands are the Debian ones. With uv you skip the separate pip step, and uv also installs the Python version you ask for. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell mkdir ml-project && cd ml-project uv venv --python 3.14 # creates .venv with Python 3.14 source .venv/bin/activate uv pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn joblib uv pip freeze > requirements.txt Run this one on your own computer. It creates a real virtual environment and starts a second python program, which the browser version of Python cannot do. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 venv_demo.py. In the exam The program shows what python -m venv creates: a bin folder (Scripts on Windows), include and lib folders, and pyvenv.cfg. The new python reports that it is inside a virtual environment, its packages go to the environment’s own site-packages, and include-system-site-packages = false means it cannot see packages installed for the whole computer. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 pin_check.py. Remember The check finds two problems: NumPy 2.4.6 is installed but the file asks for 2.3.5, and MLflow is missing. Running pip install -r requirements.txt fixes both, which is exactly what a CI job or a new teammate does first. Command pip and venv uv Create an environment python3 -m venv .venv uv venv Install packages pip install pandas uv pip install"],[0,"venv","Virtual environments and packages","pandas Install from a file pip install -r requirements.txt uv pip install -r requirements.txt Save exact versions pip freeze > requirements.txt uv pip freeze > requirements.txt Install a Python version Use the python.org installer uv python install 3.14 Lesson 4 of 9"],[0,"gitcode","Git, VS Code and Jupyter","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Pro Git, chapter 1.6 First time Git setup, and the Visual Studio Code documentation for Python and Jupyter. What it is Git records every change to your code so you can go back, branch and work with others. VS Code is a free editor with a Python extension (running, debugging, picking the environment) and a Jupyter extension for notebooks. Jupyter notebooks mix code, output and notes, which suits exploring data. Why we need it MLOps starts with version control: the training code, the configs and the requirements must all be in Git so a model can be traced back to the exact code that made it. Notebooks are great for exploring, and scripts in Git are better for anything that must run again. How it works Install Git and VS Code, set your name and email once, install the Python and Jupyter extensions, and choose your .venv as the interpreter (VS Code asks, or use the command Python: Select Interpreter). Add .venv to .gitignore. Install Git and VS Code macOS Terminal (macOS)shell git --version # came with xcode-select brew install --cask visual-studio-code # or download from code.visualstudio.com Windows 10 and 11 PowerShell (Windows)shell winget install Git.Git winget install Microsoft.VisualStudioCode # close and reopen PowerShell, then git --version Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) Terminal (Linux)shell git --version # installed with the python packages above sudo snap install code --classic # Ubuntu, or the .deb or .rpm from code.visualstudio.com ChromeOS (Chromebook) Download the .deb build of VS Code for your Chromebook’s processor (x64 for Intel and AMD, Arm64 for Arm) into Linux files, then install it. It appears in the launcher under Linux apps. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell git --version sudo apt install -y ./code_*.deb Every system, inside the project foldershell git config --global user.name \"Your Name\" git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\" git init echo \".venv/\" >> .gitignore pip install jupyterlab ipykernel # notebooks inside the project environment jupyter lab New to Git?The Git and CI/CD subject teaches commits, branches, merging and GitHub Actions step by step. Open Git and CI/CD Lesson 5 of 9"],[0,"docker","Docker on each system","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Docker documentation: Install Docker Desktop on Mac, Install Docker Desktop on Windows, Install Docker Engine (convenience script), and Microsoft Learn, How to install Linux on Windows with WSL. What it is Docker packages an app with its Python, libraries and files into an image, and runs it as a container, which behaves the same on any computer with Docker. Docker Desktop is the app for macOS and Windows. Linux runs Docker Engine directly. Why we need it A model that works on your laptop often fails on a server because of a different Python, library or system package. A container fixes that by shipping the whole environment. Chapter 2.3 of this subject builds a container for a model API. How it works Install Docker for your system as below, start it, and run the hello-world container to check. Containers are Linux programs, so macOS and Windows run them inside a small Linux virtual machine, and on Windows that is WSL 2. Install Docker macOS Install Docker Desktop for Mac, choosing the Apple silicon or Intel download, and start it once from Applications. It runs a small Linux virtual machine for the containers. Terminal (macOS)shell brew install --cask docker # or the download from docker.com docker run hello-world Windows 10 and 11 Docker on Windows runs Linux containers inside WSL 2, the Windows Subsystem for Linux. Install WSL first (it needs a restart), then Docker Desktop, and keep the WSL 2 based engine option on. PowerShell (Windows)shell wsl --install # in PowerShell as administrator, then restart winget install Docker.DockerDesktop docker run hello-world Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) On Linux you install Docker Engine directly, no virtual machine needed. Docker’s script adds its package repository and installs the engine. Adding yourself to the docker group lets you run docker without sudo after you log out and back in. Terminal (Linux)shell curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh sudo sh get-docker.sh sudo usermod -aG docker $USER # then log out and back in docker run hello-world ChromeOS (Chromebook) Docker Engine can be installed in the Linux container with the same script as Debian. It needs disk space, so raise the Linux disk size in Settings if needed. On a managed Chromebook this may be blocked, and a cloud machine or GitHub Codespaces is the alternative. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh sudo sh get-docker.sh sudo usermod -aG docker $USER docker run hello-world Remember If hello-world prints Hello from Docker!, the install works. Permission denied on /var/run/docker.sock on Linux means you have not logged out and back in since joining the docker group. Lesson 6 of 9"],[0,"gpu","GPUs, the cloud and common problems","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: PyTorch Get Started (install selector), PyTorch documentation for the MPS backend, NVIDIA CUDA on WSL user guide, and the NVIDIA Container Toolkit install guide. What it is A GPU runs thousands of small calculations at once, which is what training neural networks needs. NVIDIA GPUs use CUDA, Apple silicon uses MPS, and cloud notebooks such as Google Colab and Kaggle lend you a GPU in the browser. Why we need it Classical ML from the Machine Learning subject (scikit-learn, gradient boosting) runs well on any CPU. Deep learning on images or text can be 10 to 50 times faster on a GPU, so it decides whether an experiment takes minutes or a whole day. How it works Start on the CPU. When a model is too slow, use your own GPU if you have one (steps below) or a free cloud GPU, and only then pay for cloud machines. Use a GPU macOS Macs have no NVIDIA GPU, so CUDA is not available. On Apple silicon, PyTorch uses the built in GPU through MPS (Metal Performance Shaders): move tensors to torch.device(\"mps\"). Check with torch.backends.mps.is_available(). Windows 10 and 11 With an NVIDIA card, install the newest NVIDIA driver, then pick the CUDA build on the PyTorch install page. nvidia-smi shows the card and driver. The same driver also gives CUDA inside WSL 2, so Linux tools work too. PowerShell (Windows)shell nvidia-smi python -c \"import torch; print(torch.cuda.is_available())\" Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) Install the NVIDIA driver from your distribution (on Ubuntu: sudo ubuntu-drivers install), restart, and check with nvidia-smi. For GPUs inside Docker, add the NVIDIA Container Toolkit and run containers with --gpus all. Terminal (Linux)shell sudo ubuntu-drivers install nvidia-smi docker run --rm --gpus all ubuntu nvidia-smi # after the NVIDIA Container Toolkit ChromeOS (Chromebook) Chromebooks have no GPU access for machine learning in the Linux container. Train on the CPU for small models, and use Google Colab or Kaggle, which give free GPU time in the browser, for anything larger. Problem System Fix python: command not found macOS, Linux, ChromeOS Use python3, or activate the .venv Typing python opens the Microsoft Store Windows Turn off the App execution aliases for python Running scripts is disabled on this system Windows PowerShell Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned error: externally-managed-environment Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora, ChromeOS Install inside a virtual environment, never with sudo pip ensurepip is not available Debian, Ubuntu, ChromeOS sudo apt install python3-venv A package installed but import fails All pip and python come from different places: use python -m pip Linux option missing in Settings ChromeOS Managed device: ask the administrator or use Colab Docker permission denied Linux, ChromeOS Join the docker group, then log out and back in Lesson 7 of 9"],[0,"versus","venv, uv, conda and Docker compared","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Ways to give a project its own environment venv and pip uv conda Docker What it isolates Python packages Python packages and Python versions Python and non Python libraries The whole system: OS libraries, Python, packages Comes with Python Yes No, one small install No, a separate distribution No, a separate app Speed Fine Very fast Slower Slow to build, fast to start Lock file requirements.txt uv.lock or requirements.txt environment.yml Dockerfile plus requirements Best for Learning, small projects Everyday work, CI Science stacks with C and CUDA libraries Serving and deploying Bottom line: learn with venv and pip, work day to day with uv, reach for conda when a library needs system packages, and ship with Docker. Lesson 8 of 9"],[0,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths Install Python, open a terminal, make a folder for your project, and give it its own environment so its packages do not mix with others. Use pip or uv with requirements.txt, Git with a .gitignore, VS Code with the right interpreter, and Docker for a first container, on your own system. Pin versions with lock files, set up the same environment in CI and in containers, use GPUs locally and in the cloud, and fix PATH, permission and driver problems quickly. Design reproducible environments for a team: base images, dev containers, hermetic builds, GPU drivers in clusters, and software supply chain checks for packages. Beginner Set up Python and a project environment. Intermediate Set up a complete ML workstation on any system. Expert Make environments reproducible for a whole team. Lesson 9 of 9"],[0,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. An ML workstation needs Python 3.12 or newer, a terminal, a virtual environment per project, pip or uv, Git, an editor, and later Docker and a GPU or cloud notebook. The terminal runs the first match it finds in PATH. Activating .venv puts its folder first, so python and pip mean the project’s own copies. macOS and Linux use python3 and .venv/bin. Windows uses python or py and .venv\\Scripts. ChromeOS runs a Debian container where the Linux steps apply. Save exact versions with pip freeze > requirements.txt and rebuild with pip install -r requirements.txt. Commit requirements.txt, never .venv. Docker Desktop runs containers on macOS and Windows (through WSL 2), Docker Engine runs them natively on Linux, and hello-world checks the install. Next: MLOps 1.2 explains the MLOps lifecycle and how to make a project reproducible from data to deployment. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: reading a requirements line, checking a version against it, and finding a command on PATH.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[1,"","The MLOps lifecycle and reproducible projects","The MLOps loop and maturity levels, seeds and run records, data and model versioning, and project layout. · MLOps · lifecycle · maturity levels · reproducibility · random seed · run record · data versioning · DVC · model registry · lineage Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 1: Set up and plan›MLOps lifecycle Step 1 · Topic 2 of 2 · Beginner See the whole loop from data to monitoring at three levels of automation, then make runs repeatable with seeds and run records, version data and models, and lay out a project that can grow into production. About 45 min7 lessons2 animations3 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What MLOps is 2Reproducibility: seeds and run records 3Versioning data and models 4A project layout that grows into production 5DevOps, MLOps and DataOps compared 6From beginner to expert 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[1,"what","What MLOps is","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Google Cloud, MLOps: Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine learning, Sculley and colleagues, Hidden technical debt in machine learning systems (NeurIPS 2015), and Kreuzberger, Kuhl and Hirschl (IEEE Access 2023). The MLOps lifecycle at three levels of automationA model goes round the same loop again and again. The difference between a hobby project and a reliable product is how much of the loop runs automatically. Green stages run without a person, grey stages need one. Step 0 of 10 Maturity level Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Level 0: every stage needs a person, so the model is rarely retrained and quietly gets worse. Level 2: only defining the problem needs a person. New code and new data both flow to production through tested pipelines. What it is MLOps (machine learning operations) is the set of practices that take a model from an experiment to a product and keep it working: versioning data, code and models, automated training pipelines, testing, deployment, monitoring and retraining. It extends DevOps, which does this for ordinary software, with the parts that are special to ML. Why we need it In ML the behaviour comes from data as much as from code. A model can be correct on launch day and wrong a month later because the world changed, with no code change at all. Sculley and colleagues (2015) showed that the model code is a small box in a real ML system, surrounded by data collection, feature pipelines, serving and monitoring, and that most of the long term cost lives there. How it works Treat the loop as the product: define the problem and metric, version the data, train in a repeatable pipeline that logs everything, evaluate against the current model, register the winner, deploy it, monitor it, and retrain when monitoring says so. Automate one stage at a time, starting with training. Level What is automated Typical sign 0: manual Nothing. Notebooks, files copied by hand A model is retrained a few times a year 1: ML pipeline automation Data, training, evaluation and registration run as one pipeline, retraining is triggered Continuous training (CT) on new data 2: CI/CD pipeline automation The pipeline itself is tested, built and deployed by CI/CD Many models updated safely every week An ML systemExam favourite behaviour = code + data + model + configuration All four need versions. Continuous trainingExam favourite new data → retrain → evaluate → register → deploy The loop of level 1. Automation share automated stages / all stages A rough maturity measure. Real world scenario: A churn model that went stale A telecom company launched a model that predicted which customers would leave, with 90 percent precision. Six months later a competitor launched a cheap plan, customer behaviour changed and precision fell to 60 percent, but nobody noticed because nothing watched the model. With MLOps, a monitor would have flagged the change in input data within days and triggered retraining on recent customers. Lesson 2 of 7"],[1,"repro","Reproducibility: seeds and run records","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Pineau and colleagues, Improving reproducibility in machine learning research (JMLR 2021), the scikit-learn glossary entry for random_state, and the MLflow tracking documentation. What it is A run is reproducible when the same code, data, configuration and environment give the same result. Randomness hides in data splits, model initialisation, bagging and shuffling, and a random seed fixes it. A run record stores everything about one run: data version, parameters, metrics and package versions. Why we need it Without reproducibility you cannot tell whether a change helped, debug a model that misbehaves in production, or prove to an auditor how a decision was made. With it you can rerun any past model exactly and roll back to it. How it works Set random_state (or seed NumPy, Python and PyTorch) and keep the seed in the configuration. Report the spread over several seeds, not one lucky number. Save a run record for every training run, which tools such as MLflow do for you. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 seeds.py. In the exam The first block changes every time you run it, which is the point. With the seed fixed at 42, five runs give exactly the same accuracy, 0.972, five times. Over ten different seeds the accuracy has mean 0.956 and standard deviation 0.014, ranging from 0.937 to 0.972, so a difference of 0.01 between two models trained once means nothing. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 run_record.py. Remember The record says the wine data had 178 rows and 13 features with fingerprint 3a28849ef236, lists the parameters (C = 0.5, seed 7), the metrics (test accuracy 0.9778) and the Python, NumPy and scikit-learn versions. With this one JSON file, anyone can rebuild and check the model. Lesson 3 of 7"],[1,"versions","Versioning data and models","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: The DVC documentation (Data Version Control), Google, Rules of machine learning (rule 4 onwards), and the MLflow model registry documentation. What it is Data versioning gives every dataset a fixed identity, usually a content fingerprint (a hash), so you always know which data trained which model. Tools such as DVC keep large files outside Git and store their hashes in Git. A model registry stores each trained model with its version, metrics, data version and stage (for example staging or production). Lineage is the chain from raw data to deployed model. Why we need it Data changes silently: a new export drops rows, a labelling mistake flips classes, a column changes its units. The code stays the same but the model changes, and without versions nobody can explain why accuracy moved or go back to the last good model. How it works Hash the data at every pipeline step and save the hash in the run record. Commit small data and DVC pointer files to Git. Register every model that passes evaluation, with tags for the code commit and the data fingerprint, and promote or roll back by changing the registry stage, not by copying files. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 data_versions.py. In the exam The original breast cancer data gives a 5 fold accuracy of 0.981. Flipping 40 labels (7 percent) drops it to 0.882 with the same number of rows. Losing about 30 percent of the rows (399 left) gives 0.975. Each version has a different fingerprint, so the run record shows exactly which one a model saw. Versioning a dataset with DVC (every system)shell pip install dvc git init && dvc init dvc add data/customers.csv # writes data/customers.csv.dvc with the hash git add data/customers.csv.dvc data/.gitignore git commit -m \"Data version 1\" dvc push # uploads the file to remote storage (S3, GCS, Azure, a drive) What Versioned with Example Code and configs Git Commit a1b2c3d Data Fingerprint, DVC or lakeFS customers.csv, hash 25e2eb7b7a87 Environment requirements.txt, lock file, Docker image tag ml-api:1.4.0 Model Model registry churn-model version 7, stage production Experiments Tracking server MLflow run 3f9e, accuracy 0.981 Lesson 4 of 7"],[1,"project","A project layout that grows into production","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Cookiecutter Data Science (DrivenData), Breck and colleagues, The ML test score: a rubric for ML production readiness (2017), and Huyen, Designing machine learning systems (2022) chapter 10. What it is A good ML project separates data, notebooks for exploring, source code for anything that must run again, configs for parameters, tests, and models or reports produced by runs. Settings live in config files, not inside the code. Why we need it Notebooks are perfect for exploring and terrible for production: cells run out of order, results hide in outputs, and they are hard to test or review. Moving reusable steps into small functions in src makes them testable and lets a pipeline call them. How it works Start from a standard layout, keep raw data read only, put every step (load, clean, features, train, evaluate) in its own function, and test data and model as well as code. The ML test score asks for tests of features, data, the model and the infrastructure. A layout that works on every systemshell ml-project/ data/raw/ # never edited, versioned with DVC data/processed/ notebooks/ # exploring only src/ data.py # load and clean features.py train.py # reads configs/train.yaml, writes a run record evaluate.py configs/train.yaml tests/ test_data.py # schema, ranges, missing values test_model.py # accuracy above a floor, invariance checks models/ # outputs, not in Git requirements.txt README.md Test type Checks Example Data tests Schema, ranges, missing values, label balance age between 0 and 120 Feature tests Transformations do what they claim Scaled column has mean near 0 on training data Model tests Quality floor, behaviour on known cases Accuracy at least 0.9, prediction unchanged when the name changes Infrastructure tests Pipeline runs end to end, model loads and serves Training on 100 rows finishes, API returns a valid answer Lesson 5 of 7"],[1,"versus","DevOps, MLOps and DataOps compared","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Three operations disciplines DevOps MLOps DataOps What it ships Software Models and the pipelines that make them Data products and pipelines What changes behaviour Code Code, data and model Data and transformations Testing Unit and integration tests Plus data tests and model quality gates Data quality checks After release Uptime and errors Plus drift, accuracy and fairness Freshness and quality of data Typical tools Git, CI/CD, Docker, Kubernetes Plus MLflow, DVC, feature stores, registries Airflow, dbt, data contracts Bottom line: MLOps reuses everything from DevOps and adds versioning of data and models, training pipelines and model monitoring. Lesson 6 of 7"],[1,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths A model is not finished when it scores well once. It needs to be rebuilt, shipped, watched and improved, again and again. Fix seeds, write run records, version data by fingerprint, keep code in a clean layout with tests, and explain the three maturity levels. Build level 1 training pipelines with tracking and a registry, version data with DVC, and add data, model and infrastructure tests to CI. Design level 2 platforms: CI/CD for pipelines, feature stores, lineage across teams, governance and audit, and the cost of ML technical debt. Beginner Explain the MLOps loop. Intermediate Make experiments reproducible and versioned. Expert Design automated ML platforms. Lesson 7 of 7"],[1,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. MLOps versions, tests, deploys, monitors and retrains ML systems. It adds data and model concerns to DevOps. The lifecycle loops: problem, data, train, evaluate, register, deploy, monitor, retrain. Levels 0, 1 and 2 automate more of it. Fix random seeds, report the spread over several seeds, and save a run record with data fingerprint, parameters, metrics and versions. Version data by hash (DVC), models in a registry, code in Git and the environment in requirements or images, so every model has a lineage. Keep a clean project layout, move notebook code into tested functions, and test data, features, model and infrastructure. Next: MLOps 2.1 builds a full scikit-learn pipeline and saves it as one file ready to serve. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a data fingerprint, picking the best run, and the spread of scores over seeds.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[2,"","Pipelines and saving models","Leak free scikit-learn pipelines, saving with joblib and metadata, file formats, and input contracts. · Pipeline · ColumnTransformer · data leakage · joblib · model persistence · ONNX · skops · input validation · handle_unknown · metadata Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 2: Build and ship›Pipelines and saving Step 2 · Topic 1 of 3 · Intermediate Put every preprocessing step and the model into one scikit-learn pipeline, keep it free of leakage, save it with its metadata, and check every input against a contract before it reaches the model. About 45 min7 lessons2 animations3 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1One pipeline from raw columns to prediction 2Leakage: why every learning step belongs inside the pipeline 3Saving and loading a trained pipeline 4An input contract for the model 5Ways to save a model compared 6From beginner to expert 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[2,"pipe","One pipeline from raw columns to prediction","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: scikit-learn user guide, 6.1 Pipelines and composite estimators (Pipeline, ColumnTransformer) and 6.3 Preprocessing data. A pipeline learns on training rows, then applies the same steps to every new rowThe pipeline has three steps before the model: fill a missing tenure with the median, scale it to a z score, and one hot encode the plan. fit learns the median, mean, spread and list of plans from the training rows only. predict reuses them. Step 0 of 9 New customer Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this A missing tenure: the imputer fills in the training median, 14, so the model still gets a number. An unseen plan: with handle_unknown set to ignore, every plan column becomes 0 instead of crashing the service. What it is A Pipeline chains preprocessing steps and a model into one object with fit and predict. A ColumnTransformer sends different columns to different steps, for example imputing and scaling the numbers while one hot encoding the categories. Each step fits on training data (learns a median, a mean, a list of categories) and then transforms any data with what it learned. Why we need it Without a pipeline, the same preprocessing is written twice: once in the training notebook and again in the serving code, and the two copies drift apart. A pipeline keeps the whole recipe in one object that is trained, saved, loaded and served together, and it makes cross validation honest because every step refits inside each fold. How it works List the numeric and categorical columns, build one small pipeline for each kind, combine them in a ColumnTransformer, and put the model last. Set handle_unknown='ignore' on the encoder so a category never seen in training does not crash the service. Call fit once on the training data, and only predict afterwards. Python # One object for the whole recipe: fill gaps, scale numbers, one hot encode categories, then the model.import numpy as npimport pandas as pdfrom sklearn.compose importColumnTransformerfrom sklearn.ensemble importHistGradientBoostingClassifierfrom sklearn.impute importSimpleImputerfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score, train_test_splitfrom sklearn.pipeline importPipelinefrom sklearn.preprocessing importOneHotEncoder, StandardScalerrng = np.random.default_rng(1)n = 1000df = pd.DataFrame({'tenure_months': rng.integers(1, 72, n).astype(float),'monthly_bill': rng.normal(60, 20, n).round(2),'support_calls': rng.poisson(1.5, n).astype(float),'plan': rng.choice(['basic', 'plus', 'premium'], n),'payment': rng.choice(['card', 'bank', 'cash'], n),})risk = -0.04 * df.tenure_months + 0.5 * df.support_calls + 0.8 * (df.plan == 'basic') + 0.02 * df.monthly_bill - 1df['churned'] = (rng.random(n) < 1 / (1 + np.exp(-risk))).astype(int)df.loc[rng.random(n) < 0.05, 'monthly_bill'] = np.nan # 5 percent of bills are missingnumeric = ['tenure_months', 'monthly_bill', 'support_calls']categorical = ['plan', 'payment']prep = ColumnTransformer([ ('num', Pipeline([('impute', SimpleImputer(strategy='median')), ('scale', StandardScaler())]), numeric), ('cat', OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown='ignore'), categorical),])model = Pipeline([('prep', prep), ('clf', HistGradientBoostingClassifier(max_iter=200, learning_rate=0.05, random_state=0))])X, y = df[numeric + categorical], df['churned']X_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=0, stratify=y)print('5 fold ROC AUC on training data:', round(cross_val_score(model, X_tr, y_tr, cv=5, scoring='roc_auc').mean(), 3))model.fit(X_tr, y_tr)print('Test accuracy:', round(model.score(X_te, y_te), 3))print('Columns after preprocessing:', list(model.named_steps['prep'].get_feature_names_out()))new = pd.DataFrame([{'tenure_months': 3, 'monthly_bill': np.nan, 'support_calls': 4, 'plan': 'student', 'payment': 'card'}])print('A new customer with a missing bill"],[2,"pipe","One pipeline from raw columns to prediction","and an unseen plan \"student\":')print(' churn probability', round(model.predict_proba(new)[0, 1], 3)) Output 5 fold ROC AUC on training data: 0.751 Test accuracy: 0.69 Columns after preprocessing: ['num__tenure_months', 'num__monthly_bill', 'num__support_calls', 'cat__plan_basic', 'cat__plan_plus', 'cat__plan_premium', 'cat__payment_bank', 'cat__payment_card', 'cat__payment_cash'] A new customer with a missing bill and an unseen plan \"student\": churn probability 0.782 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 column_pipeline.py. In the exam The churn pipeline reaches a 5 fold ROC AUC of 0.751 and a test accuracy of 0.69. After preprocessing, 5 raw columns have become 9 (3 scaled numbers and 6 one hot columns, with names such as cat__plan_basic). A new customer with a missing bill and a plan called student, which the model never saw, still gets a churn probability of 0.782 instead of an error. Standard scalingExam favourite z = (x − μtrain) / σtrain Statistics from the training data only. Median imputationExam favourite missing x → median of training x Robust to outliers. One hot columnsExam favourite one column per category seen in training Unknown category → all zeros with ignore. Want the preprocessing steps in depth?The Machine Learning subject, Foundations 3.1 and 3.2, explains cleaning, imputing, scaling and encoding with animations. Open Machine Learning Lesson 2 of 7"],[2,"leak","Leakage: why every learning step belongs inside the pipeline","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Hastie, Tibshirani and Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning (2009) section 7.10.2, Kaufman and colleagues, Leakage in data mining (2012), and scikit-learn user guide section 11.2, Data leakage. What it is Data leakage is when information that will not be available at prediction time gets into training or evaluation. A common form is preprocessing or feature selection fitted on all the rows before splitting, so the test rows influence the model that is then tested on them. Why we need it Leakage gives scores that look excellent in the notebook and collapse in production. It is one of the most common reasons ML projects fail, because nothing crashes: the numbers are simply wrong. How it works Split first. Put every step that learns from data (imputers, scalers, encoders, feature selection, resampling) inside the pipeline, and cross validate the whole pipeline. Also check features for values recorded after the event you predict, such as a cancellation date in a churn model. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 leakage_pipeline.py. In the exam On 100 rows of random noise with random labels, choosing the 20 best of 5000 columns before cross validation reports an accuracy of 0.85. Doing the same selection inside the pipeline reports 0.48, the honest answer for labels that are pure chance. Kind of leakage Example Fix Preprocessing on all rows Scaler fitted before the split Scaler inside the pipeline Selection on all rows Top correlated columns chosen with test rows SelectKBest inside the pipeline Target leakage A column filled in after the outcome is known Remove it, check the time each column becomes available Time leakage Random split of time ordered data Split by time: train on the past, test on the future Group leakage The same patient in train and test GroupKFold by patient Lesson 3 of 7"],[2,"save","Saving and loading a trained pipeline","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: scikit-learn user guide, 10 Model persistence, the joblib documentation, the skops documentation, and the ONNX and sklearn-onnx documentation. What it is Serialising a model writes the fitted object to a file so another program can load it and predict. scikit-learn models are usually saved with joblib, which uses Python’s pickle format and handles NumPy arrays efficiently. A small metadata file next to it records the features, classes and library versions. Why we need it Training happens once, prediction happens millions of times in another process, often on another machine. The file is the hand over between the two. The metadata makes sure the loading side uses compatible versions and sends the features in the right order. How it works Save the whole pipeline, never just the model, with joblib.dump, and load it with joblib.load in the serving code. Load only files you trust, because loading a pickle can run code. Use the same scikit-learn version to load. For safer or cross language formats, use skops or convert to ONNX. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 save_load.py. In the exam The fitted iris pipeline takes about 1.5 KB on disk. After loading, its predictions match the original on every row, and the flower [5.9, 3.0, 5.1, 1.8] is predicted as virginica. The JSON file records scikit-learn 1.8.0, which the serving side should match. Every system, inside the activated .venvshell pip install joblib skops skl2onnx onnxruntime python train.py # writes models/churn.joblib and models/churn.json git add models/churn.json # metadata in Git, the binary in a model registry or DVC Lesson 4 of 7"],[2,"contract","An input contract for the model","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Breck and colleagues, The ML test score (2017), data and feature tests, Google, Rules of machine learning rule 10, and the pydantic documentation. What it is An input contract (or schema) says which fields a request must have, their types and their allowed ranges. Requests that break it are rejected with a clear message, in a web API usually with status code 422 (unprocessable content), before they reach the model. Why we need it A model will happily return a number for nonsense input: a negative age, a price sent as text, a missing field silently filled with a default. Those wrong answers flow into decisions. Checking inputs turns silent errors into loud, fixable ones. How it works Write the schema once, check every field for presence, type and range, warn about unknown categories, and reject extra fields. In FastAPI, a pydantic model does this automatically, as chapter 2.2 shows. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 input_check.py. Remember The first request passes. The second is rejected because tenure is −3 and the bill was sent as text. The third has four problems: a missing bill, 2.5 support calls where a whole number is required, an unknown plan, and an unexpected coupon field. Lesson 5 of 7"],[2,"versus","Ways to save a model compared","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Model file formats joblib (pickle) skops ONNX Framework format What it stores Any Python object scikit-learn objects A computation graph PyTorch state_dict, Keras .keras Safe to load from strangers No, can run code Yes, checks types first Yes Mostly (PyTorch weights_only loading) Needs Python to serve Yes, same versions Yes No: C++, C#, Java, JavaScript runtimes Usually Speed of serving Normal Normal Often faster with ONNX Runtime Normal Best for Your own models, quick start Sharing scikit-learn models Serving across languages and devices Deep learning models Bottom line: joblib for your own Python services, skops when files come from elsewhere, ONNX when the serving side is not Python. Lesson 6 of 7"],[2,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths Put all the cleaning steps and the model into one box, train the box, and save it as one file. Build Pipelines with ColumnTransformer, avoid leakage, save with joblib plus metadata, and validate every input. Write custom transformers, tune whole pipelines with grid search, handle time and group splits, and export to skops or ONNX. Design feature stores that share one feature definition between training and serving, detect training serving skew, and secure the model supply chain. Beginner Train and save a pipeline. Intermediate Build leak free pipelines that serve safely. Expert Keep features identical in training and production. Lesson 7 of 7"],[2,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. A Pipeline plus a ColumnTransformer holds imputing, scaling, encoding and the model in one object with fit and predict. Steps fit on training data only and transform new rows with what they learned. handle_unknown='ignore' keeps unseen categories from crashing. Leakage: any step fitted on all rows (scaling, selection) lets test rows shape the model. Selection outside cross validation scored 0.85 on noise, inside it scored 0.48. Save the whole pipeline with joblib plus a metadata file with features, classes and versions. Load only trusted files, with the same library versions. Check every request against an input contract and reject bad ones with status 422. Next: MLOps 2.2 serves the saved pipeline as a web API with Flask, FastAPI and Django. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: median imputation, z scores with training statistics, and one hot encoding with unknown categories.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[3,"","Serving models with Flask, FastAPI and Django","Model APIs in Flask, FastAPI and Django, calling them on each OS, online against batch, and production servers sized with Little's law. · model serving · Flask · FastAPI · Django · REST API · batch prediction · online prediction · Gunicorn · Waitress · Uvicorn · Little's law Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 2: Build and ship›Serving models Step 2 · Topic 2 of 3 · Intermediate Turn a saved pipeline into a web API with Flask, FastAPI and Django, call it from every operating system, choose between online and batch prediction, and run it on a production server with enough workers. About 55 min8 lessons2 animations5 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1A model behind a web API 2FastAPI: the input contract written as a class 3Django: when the model joins a full web application 4Online against batch prediction 5From the development server to production 6Flask, FastAPI, Django and model servers compared 7From beginner to expert 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[3,"api","A model behind a web API","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Flask documentation (Quickstart and Testing), RFC 9110 HTTP Semantics (methods and status codes), and Chip Huyen, Designing Machine Learning Systems (2022) chapter 7. One request through a model APIA client (a website, an app or another service) sends JSON over HTTP. The web framework routes it to a function, the input is checked, the pipeline that was loaded once at start up predicts, and JSON goes back with a status code. Step 0 of 6 Request Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this A word instead of a number: validation stops it with status 422 before the model is called. GET instead of POST: the route only accepts POST, so the framework answers 405 without running your code. What it is A model API is a small web service. A client sends an HTTP request with JSON, the service checks it, runs the saved pipeline and sends back a JSON response with a status code. A route such as POST /predict connects a URL and a method to one Python function. Why we need it A model inside a notebook helps one person. Behind an API, any website, phone app or other service can use it in any programming language, and you can update the model without touching those clients. How it works Load the pipeline once when the server starts. Add a /health route that says the service is alive, and a /predict route that validates the input, predicts and returns JSON. Test it in the same process with the framework’s test client before starting a real server. Status Meaning When a model API sends it 200 OK Success The prediction is in the body 400 Bad Request The request itself is broken The body is not valid JSON at all 405 Method Not Allowed Wrong method for this route GET sent to a POST only route 422 Unprocessable Content Valid JSON but the content fails the checks A word instead of a number, a missing field 500 Internal Server Error A bug or crash inside the service Never on purpose. Log it and fix it. Python # Serve a model with Flask (pip install flask scikit-learn).# python flask_serve.py tries the API in this process with Flask's test client# python flask_serve.py serve starts the server on http://127.0.0.1:8000, call it with curl (see the lesson)import sysfrom flask importFlask, jsonify, requestfrom sklearn.datasets import load_irisfrom sklearn.linear_model importLogisticRegressionfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipelinefrom sklearn.preprocessing importStandardScaler# In a real service: model = joblib.load('models/iris.joblib'), done once when the app startsX, y = load_iris(return_X_y=True)model = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression(max_iter=500)).fit(X, y)CLASSES = ['setosa', 'versicolor', 'virginica']app = Flask(__name__)@app.get('/health')def health():return {'status': 'ok'}@app.post('/predict')def predict(): body = request.get_json(silent=True) or {} features = body.get('features')ifnot isinstance(features, list) orlen(features) != 4ornot all(isinstance(v, (int, float)) for v in features):return jsonify(error='send {\"features\": [four numbers]}'), 422 probs = model.predict_proba([features])[0] best = int(probs.argmax())return {'class': CLASSES[best], 'probability': round(float(probs[best]), 3)}if __name__ == '__main__':if'serve'in sys.argv: app.run(port=8000) # development server only, see the lesson for productionelse: # try the API with Flask's test client, no network needed client = app.test_client()print('GET /health ', client.get('/health').get_json()) r = client.post('/predict', json={'features': [5.9, 3.0, 5.1, 1.8]})print('POST /predict ', r.status_code, r.get_json()) r = client.post('/predict', json={'features': [5.9, 'wide']})print('POST /predict ', r.status_code, r.get_json()) Output GET /health {'status': 'ok'} POST /predict 200 {'class': 'virginica', 'probability': 0.703} POST /predict 422 {'error': 'send {\"features\": [four numbers]}'} Run this"],[3,"api","A model behind a web API","one on your own computer. It needs Flask, FastAPI or Django, which the browser version of Python cannot install or run as a web server. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 flask_serve.py. Remember The test client sends real requests to the app inside one Python process, so no port or network is needed. The good request gets 200 with virginica at 0.703. The broken one gets 422 and an error that tells the caller what to send. The model never sees bad data. Start the server and call it from a second terminal macOS Open Terminal, go to your project folder and activate its environment (MLOps 1.1). Install the packages and start the Flask server. It keeps running and prints each request it receives. Open a second Terminal window (Command and N) and call the API with curl, which macOS already has. Stop the server with Control and C in the first window. Terminal 1 (macOS)shell cd ml-project && source .venv/bin/activate pip install flask scikit-learn python flask_serve.py serve # Running on http://127.0.0.1:8000 Terminal 2 (macOS)shell curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"features\": [5.9, 3.0, 5.1, 1.8]}' The examples use port 8000 on purpose. On macOS 12 and later the AirPlay Receiver already listens on port 5000, the old Flask default, and answers 403 Forbidden. If you ever need port 5000, turn AirPlay Receiver off in System Settings, General, AirDrop and Handoff. To see what holds a port, run lsof -i :8000. Windows 10 and 11 Open PowerShell, go to your project folder and activate its environment (MLOps 1.1). Install the packages and start the server. If Windows Defender Firewall asks about Python, allow it on private networks only. The server listens on 127.0.0.1, so nothing outside your computer can reach it. Open a second PowerShell window and call the API. Invoke-RestMethod is built in and turns the JSON answer into an object. Stop the server with Control and C. PowerShell 1 (Windows)shell cd ml-project .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 pip install flask scikit-learn python flask_serve.py serve PowerShell 2 (Windows)shell Invoke-RestMethod http://127.0.0.1:8000/health Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post -Uri http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict -ContentType 'application/json' -Body '{\"features\": [5.9, 3.0, 5.1, 1.8]}' # curl also ships with Windows 10 and 11. Type curl.exe, because in Windows PowerShell 5.1 # the word curl is a different command (an alias of Invoke-WebRequest). curl.exe http://127.0.0.1:8000/health To see what holds a port, run netstat -ano | findstr :8000. The last column is the process id, which Task Manager shows on its Details tab. Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) The same steps as macOS. Most distributions include curl. If not, install it with sudo apt install curl (Ubuntu, Debian) or sudo dnf install curl (Fedora). Terminal 1 (Linux)shell cd ml-project && source .venv/bin/activate pip install flask scikit-learn python flask_serve.py serve Terminal 2 (Linux)shell curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"features\": [5.9, 3.0, 5.1, 1.8]}' # what holds port 8000 ss -ltnp | grep 8000 ChromeOS (Chromebook) Run both terminals inside the Linux development environment (open the Terminal app, then the penguin tab, and press Control, Shift and T for a second tab). The commands are the Debian ones. Terminal tab 1 (ChromeOS Linux)shell sudo apt install curl # once, if curl is missing cd ml-project && source .venv/bin/activate pip install flask scikit-learn python flask_serve.py serve Terminal tab 2 (ChromeOS Linux)shell curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"features\": [5.9, 3.0, 5.1, 1.8]}' ChromeOS forwards localhost ports from Linux to the Chrome browser, so http://localhost:8000/health opens in a normal Chrome"],[3,"api","A model behind a web API","tab. The Port forwarding page in the Linux development environment settings (search Settings for Port forwarding) is only needed when another device on your network should reach the server. LatencyExam favourite time from request sent to response received Report the median and the 95th or 99th percentile, not only the average. Throughput of one workerExam favourite requests per second = 1000 / latency in ms A worker that needs 20 ms serves at most 50 requests per second. New to Flask, Django or web APIs?The Complete Python subject teaches Flask, Django and building REST APIs step by step, from routes and templates to JSON and testing. Open Complete Python Lesson 2 of 8"],[3,"fastapi","FastAPI: the input contract written as a class","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: FastAPI documentation: Request Body, Testing, and the automatic interactive docs. Pydantic documentation: Fields and validation errors. What it is FastAPI is a web framework built on type hints. You describe the input as a Pydantic class, for example four floats that must be above 0 and below 20, and FastAPI checks every request against it, converts the types, and writes the error messages for you. It also builds interactive documentation at /docs. Why we need it The input contract from MLOps 2.1 becomes a few lines instead of a page of if statements. The documentation always matches the code, because it is generated from the same class. FastAPI also supports async code for services that wait on databases or other APIs. How it works Write one class for the input and one for the output, and use them as the type of the function argument and as response_model. Accept a list for a batch route so many rows go through the model in one call. Run it with Uvicorn. Run this one on your own computer. It needs Flask, FastAPI or Django, which the browser version of Python cannot install or run as a web server. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 fastapi_serve.py. In the exam One bad request produced three precise errors at once: sepal_length must be greater than 0, sepal_width is not a number, and petal_width is missing. The batch route scored two flowers in a single predict_proba call, virginica at 0.703 and setosa at 0.981. Terminal (macOS, Linux, ChromeOS and PowerShell on Windows)shell pip install \"fastapi[standard]\" scikit-learn uvicorn fastapi_serve:app --port 8000 # then open http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs in the browser and press Try it out The JSON body for FastAPI uses named fields: {\"sepal_length\": 5.9, \"sepal_width\": 3.0, \"petal_length\": 5.1, \"petal_width\": 1.8}. The same curl and Invoke-RestMethod commands from the Flask lesson work with this body. Going deeper: Sync or async route functions A plain def route runs in a thread pool, which suits scikit-learn because predict is ordinary CPU work. An async def route runs on the event loop, so a slow predict inside it blocks every other request on that worker. Use async def only when the function awaits network or database calls, and keep heavy CPU work in plain def routes or in separate worker processes. Lesson 3 of 8"],[3,"django","Django: when the model joins a full web application","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Django documentation: Using settings without DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE (settings.configure), the test client, CSRF protection, and How to deploy with WSGI. What it is Django is a full web framework with a database layer, an admin site, user accounts, forms and security features. A JSON view returns a JsonResponse. The program here fits a whole Django service in one file by calling settings.configure instead of creating a project with many files. Why we need it Choose Django when the model is one feature of a larger site that already needs logins, a database and an admin, for example a clinic portal that shows a risk score next to each patient. For a model that only answers predictions, Flask or FastAPI is smaller. How it works Write a view that reads request.body, validates it, predicts and returns JsonResponse. Mark it @require_POST so other methods get 405. Mark it @csrf_exempt only because programs, not browser forms, call it, and protect it with an API key or token instead. Run this one on your own computer. It needs Flask, FastAPI or Django, which the browser version of Python cannot install or run as a web server. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 django_serve.py. Common mistake Django gives the same answers as Flask (200 with virginica at 0.703, 422 for bad JSON), and @require_POST turns a GET into 405 without running the view. In a real project the view lives in an app inside django-admin startproject, and the settings come from environment variables, never a SECRET_KEY written in the code. Terminal (every system, use PowerShell on Windows)shell pip install django scikit-learn python django_serve.py runserver 8000 # development server only Lesson 4 of 8"],[3,"modes","Online against batch prediction","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Chip Huyen, Designing Machine Learning Systems (2022) chapter 7, Model deployment and prediction service, and the pandas documentation for read_csv with chunksize. What it is Online (real time) prediction answers each request the moment it arrives, through an API like the ones above. Batch prediction runs on a schedule, scores a whole table at once and stores the answers for the application to look up later. Why we need it Online gives fresh answers during the user’s visit but needs a server running all the time and fast features. Batch is cheaper and simpler, and one predict call on many rows is far faster than many calls on one row each, but the answers are only as fresh as the last run. How it works Ask how soon the answer is needed. During the visit (fraud checks, search ranking) means online. By tomorrow (churn lists, weekly demand forecasts, email campaigns) means batch. Read big files in chunks so memory stays small. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 batch_predict.py. Remember 20,000 rows are scored in 4 chunks of 5,000. The last line times both styles on your own device, so the numbers change from run to run, but one call per row is always many times slower because each call pays the same fixed overhead. Real world scenario: A bank fraud check and a churn list A card payment must be approved or stopped within about 100 ms, so the fraud model runs online behind an API. The same bank also wants a list of customers likely to close their accounts. Nobody needs that list within 100 ms, so a batch job scores every customer at 2 am and writes the result to a table that the retention team reads in the morning. Lesson 5 of 8"],[3,"production","From the development server to production","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Flask documentation, Deploying to Production. FastAPI documentation, Deployment concepts and Server Workers. Gunicorn, Waitress and Uvicorn documentation. Little, A proof for the queuing formula L = λW (1961). What it is app.run() and runserver are development servers: one process, made for debugging, and the docs say not to use them in production. A production server (Gunicorn or Waitress for Flask and Django, which speak WSGI, and Uvicorn for FastAPI, which speaks ASGI) runs several workers and restarts any that crash. Why we need it One development server handles requests slowly and stops at the first crash. Workers serve requests in parallel, and a reverse proxy in front adds HTTPS, timeouts and limits on request size. How it works Pick the server for your system below. Start with 2 workers per CPU core as a first guess, then measure. Each worker loads its own copy of the model, so memory grows with the number of workers. Run a production server macOS Gunicorn runs Flask and Django with several worker processes. Uvicorn runs FastAPI and has its own --workers option. Both work on macOS and Linux. Terminal (macOS)shell pip install gunicorn \"uvicorn[standard]\" gunicorn -w 2 -b 127.0.0.1:8000 flask_serve:app # Flask: file flask_serve.py, object app gunicorn -w 2 -b 127.0.0.1:8000 django_serve:application # Django: the WSGI application object uvicorn fastapi_serve:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --workers 2 # FastAPI Windows 10 and 11 Gunicorn does not run on Windows, because it depends on Unix process features. Use Waitress, a pure Python production server, for Flask and Django. Uvicorn works on Windows as it is. A second choice is to run the Linux commands inside WSL (MLOps 1.1), or inside Docker, which a coming chapter covers. PowerShell (Windows)shell pip install waitress \"uvicorn[standard]\" waitress-serve --listen=127.0.0.1:8000 flask_serve:app # Flask waitress-serve --listen=127.0.0.1:8000 django_serve:application # Django uvicorn fastapi_serve:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --workers 2 # FastAPI Waitress uses threads instead of processes (4 by default, change it with --threads=8). Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) The same commands as macOS. On a Linux server, a systemd service keeps the command running and restarts it after a crash or a reboot. The more common choice today is a Docker container, which a coming chapter covers. Terminal (Linux)shell pip install gunicorn \"uvicorn[standard]\" gunicorn -w 2 -b 127.0.0.1:8000 flask_serve:app gunicorn -w 2 -b 127.0.0.1:8000 django_serve:application uvicorn fastapi_serve:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --workers 2 ChromeOS (Chromebook) Use the Linux commands inside the Linux development environment. A Chromebook is fine for learning and testing, but a real service runs on a server or in the cloud, usually in a Docker container. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell pip install gunicorn \"uvicorn[standard]\" gunicorn -w 2 -b 127.0.0.1:8000 flask_serve:app A checklist before real users call the API Load the model once at start up and keep it in memory. Keep a /health route for the load balancer, and return the model version in each response. Validate every input and cap the request size. Require an API key or token, and rate limit each caller. Put HTTPS in front with a reverse proxy such as nginx or Caddy, or the cloud’s load balancer. Log each request with its latency and status code, without personal data. Set a timeout so one slow request cannot hold a worker forever. Little’s lawExam favourite L = λ × W L requests in progress, λ arrivals per second, W seconds each request spends in the system. Workers neededExam favourite workers = λ × latency / target use Aim for about 70 to 80 percent use so short bursts do not build a queue. Waiting time for one worker (M/M/1 queue) W = 1 / (μ − λ) μ requests per second the worker can serve. W shoots up as λ nears μ. In the exam A queue does not grow in a straight line. One"],[3,"production","From the development server to production","worker that can serve 50 requests per second answers in 25 ms at 10 per second, 100 ms at 40 per second, and 1 full second at 49 per second. That is why services keep spare capacity. Lesson 6 of 8"],[3,"versus","Flask, FastAPI, Django and model servers compared","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Ways to serve a model Flask FastAPI Django Model servers (BentoML, TorchServe, KServe) Size Small and simple Small, typed Large, everything included A platform Input checks Write them yourself Automatic from Pydantic classes Forms or Django REST framework serializers Built in per model API docs Add a package Automatic at /docs Add a package Usually automatic Production server Gunicorn, Waitress Uvicorn Gunicorn, Waitress Included Extras Many plugins Async, dependency injection Database, admin, users Batching requests, GPUs, scaling, model versions Best for Learning, small services New model APIs A model inside a larger web app Many models or heavy deep learning traffic Bottom line: Start with FastAPI for a new model API, use Django when the model joins an existing Django site, and move to a model server when you run many models or need GPUs and request batching. Lesson 7 of 8"],[3,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths Put the model in a small program that waits for questions over the internet and answers each one with a prediction. Build /health and /predict routes in Flask, FastAPI or Django, validate inputs, return the right status codes, and test with the test client. Run production servers with workers, size them with Little’s law, choose online or batch prediction, and secure the API with keys, rate limits and HTTPS. Design serving systems with dynamic request batching, model servers on GPUs, canary releases, tail latency targets and autoscaling from queue length. Beginner Serve a model and call it with curl. Intermediate Ship a validated API on a production server. Expert Plan capacity and latency for real traffic. Lesson 8 of 8"],[3,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. A model API loads the saved pipeline once and answers POST /predict with JSON and a status code: 200 for success, 422 for bad content, 405 for the wrong method. Flask is small and simple. FastAPI checks inputs from Pydantic classes and writes docs at /docs. Django fits when the model joins a full web app. Test with the framework’s test client, then call the running server with curl, or with Invoke-RestMethod in Windows PowerShell. Online prediction answers during the visit. Batch prediction scores everything on a schedule, cheaper but less fresh. Production uses Gunicorn (macOS, Linux), Waitress (Windows, any system) or Uvicorn (FastAPI, any system) with several workers, sized with Little’s law. Next: Coming next: putting this service in a Docker container and testing and shipping it with CI/CD. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: validate a request body, size a pool of workers, and split a big file into chunks.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[4,"","Docker and CI/CD for models","A real Dockerfile and Compose file for a model API, per OS build steps, model behaviour tests and a GitHub Actions pipeline. · Docker · Dockerfile · container · docker compose · CI/CD · GitHub Actions · model testing · quality gate · smoke test · container registry Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 2: Build and ship›Docker and CI/CD Step 2 · Topic 3 of 3 · Intermediate Package the model API in a Docker image that runs the same on macOS, Windows, Linux and ChromeOS, test the model itself with a quality gate and behaviour tests, and run everything in GitHub Actions on every push. About 60 min7 lessons2 animations2 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Containers, images and the Dockerfile 2Build and run the model container on your computer 3Testing a model, not only the code 4CI/CD with GitHub Actions 5Virtual environment, container or virtual machine 6From beginner to expert 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[4,"docker","Containers, images and the Dockerfile","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Docker documentation: Dockerfile reference, Build cache, and Best practices for writing Dockerfiles. What it is A Docker image is a sealed package with an operating system layer, Python, the pinned packages, your code and the trained model. A container is a running copy of an image. A Dockerfile is the recipe that builds the image, one instruction per line, and a registry such as Docker Hub or GitHub Container Registry stores images so servers can pull them. Why we need it A virtual environment pins Python packages, but not the operating system, the system libraries or the Python build. An image pins all of it, so the model runs the same on your laptop, in CI and on the server, and on macOS, Windows, Linux and ChromeOS alike. How it works Start from a small official base image, copy and install the requirements first, then copy the code. Train or copy the model, switch to a user that is not root, open the port, add a health check and say which command starts the server. Word What it is Command Image A read only package built from a Dockerfile, made of layers docker build -t iris-api:1.0.0 . Container A running copy of an image, with its own file system and network docker run -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 Tag A name and version for an image iris-api:1.0.0 Registry A store for images that servers pull from docker push ghcr.io/owner/iris-api:1.0.0 Port mapping Connects a port on your computer to a port in the container -p 8000:8000 means computer 8000 to container 8000 Dockerfiledockerfile 1# A small image for the model API2FROM python:3.14-slim34# No .pyc files, and print logs straight away5ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 PYTHONUNBUFFERED=16WORKDIR /app78# Dependencies first, so this slow layer is reused while only the code changes9COPY requirements.txt .10RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt1112# Then the code, and train the model inside the image13ARG MODEL_VERSION=1.0.014ENV MODEL_VERSION=$MODEL_VERSION15COPY train.py app.py ./16RUN python train.py1718# Do not run as root19RUN useradd --create-home appuser20USER appuser2122EXPOSE 800023HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s CMD python -c \"import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8000/health')\"24CMD [\"uvicorn\", \"app:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\", \"--workers\", \"2\"] Line 2 The official slim Python image: Debian with Python 3.14 and little else. Alpine is smaller but uses musl instead of glibc, so scientific packages may need compiling. Line 5 Do not write .pyc files, and send print output straight to the logs. Line 6 Every later path is inside /app. Line 9 Only requirements.txt first. This layer changes rarely. Line 10 The slow step. Because it comes before the code, Docker reuses it from the cache after a code change. Line 13 A value you can pass at build time with --build-arg, here the model version. Line 15 The code comes after the packages. Line 16 Train inside the image, so the model and the library versions always match. Bigger models are trained elsewhere and copied in. Line 19 Create a normal user. Line 20 Run the server as that user, not as root, so a bug in the app cannot take over the container. Line 22 Documents the port the app listens on. Line 23 Docker calls /health every 30 seconds and marks the container healthy or unhealthy. The slim image has no curl, so Python does the call. Line 24 The command that starts when the container starts: Uvicorn with 2 workers on all network interfaces inside the container. The Docker layer cacheEach Dockerfile instruction makes one layer. On a rebuild Docker reuses a layer from its cache unless the instruction or the files it copies changed. Once one layer runs again, every layer above it runs again too. The times come from real builds of this chapter’s project. Step 0 of 9 Dockerfile order You changed Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new"],[4,"docker","Containers, images and the Dockerfile","piece and explains it here. Try this Bad order and a one line code change: COPY . . sees a changed file, so pip install runs again for about 18 seconds. Changing requirements.txt rebuilds pip install in both orders. That is expected, because the packages really changed. Python # Which Dockerfile steps run again after a change? A small model of Docker's layer cache.# A step runs again if the files it copies changed, or if any step before it ran again.# Step times are seconds from real builds of the chapter's project.GOOD = [('FROM python:3.14-slim', [], 0), ('WORKDIR /app', [], 0), ('COPY requirements.txt .', ['requirements.txt'], 0), ('RUN pip install -r requirements.txt', [], 18), ('COPY train.py app.py ./', ['train.py', 'app.py'], 0), ('RUN python train.py', [], 1.6), ('RUN useradd appuser', [], 0.1)]BAD = [('FROM python:3.14-slim', [], 0), ('WORKDIR /app', [], 0), ('COPY . .', ['requirements.txt', 'train.py', 'app.py'], 0.4), ('RUN pip install -r requirements.txt', [], 18.6), ('RUN python train.py', [], 1.2)]def rebuild(steps, changed): again, total = False, 0.0for instruction, files, seconds in steps:if any(f in changed for f in files): again = Trueif again: total += secondsprint(f' {\"runs again\" if again else \"CACHED \"} {instruction}')return totalfor name, steps in [('Good order', GOOD), ('Bad order', BAD)]:for changed in [{'app.py'}, {'requirements.txt'}]:print(f'{name}, changed {sorted(changed)[0]}:')print(f' about {rebuild(steps, changed):.1f} s of steps ran again') Output Good order, changed app.py: CACHED FROM python:3.14-slim CACHED WORKDIR /app CACHED COPY requirements.txt . CACHED RUN pip install -r requirements.txt runs again COPY train.py app.py ./ runs again RUN python train.py runs again RUN useradd appuser about 1.7 s of steps ran again Good order, changed requirements.txt: CACHED FROM python:3.14-slim CACHED WORKDIR /app runs again COPY requirements.txt . runs again RUN pip install -r requirements.txt runs again COPY train.py app.py ./ runs again RUN python train.py runs again RUN useradd appuser about 19.7 s of steps ran again Bad order, changed app.py: CACHED FROM python:3.14-slim CACHED WORKDIR /app runs again COPY . . runs again RUN pip install -r requirements.txt runs again RUN python train.py about 20.2 s of steps ran again Bad order, changed requirements.txt: CACHED FROM python:3.14-slim CACHED WORKDIR /app runs again COPY . . runs again RUN pip install -r requirements.txt runs again RUN python train.py about 20.2 s of steps ran again Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 layer_cache.py. In the exam Real timings of this project: with the good order, a one line change in app.py rebuilt in 2.2 seconds in total, because pip install came from the cache. With COPY . . before pip install, the same change took 32 seconds, because the 18 second install ran again. Lesson 2 of 7"],[4,"build","Build and run the model container on your computer","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Docker documentation: Docker Desktop for Mac and for Windows (WSL 2 backend), Docker Engine for Ubuntu, Debian and Fedora, Post installation steps for Linux, and the Compose file reference. What it is One download, make_project.py, creates the whole project of this chapter: the training script, the FastAPI app, the tests, the requirements, the Dockerfile, the Compose file and the GitHub Actions workflow. Why we need it Running the real thing once on your own computer makes every later idea concrete: images, containers, ports, health checks and logs. How it works Run make_project.py, build the image, start a container in the background, call it, read its logs, and stop it. Then do the same with one Compose command. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 make_project.py. Build the image and run the container macOS Start Docker Desktop (installed in MLOps 1.1) and wait until the whale icon in the menu bar says it is running. Download make_project.py from this lesson into your project folder and run the commands below in Terminal. Open http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs in the browser to try the API by clicking. Terminal (macOS)shell python3 make_project.py # creates the folder iris-api cd iris-api docker build -t iris-api:1.0.0 . docker run -d --name api -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"sepal_length\": 5.9, \"sepal_width\": 3.0, \"petal_length\": 5.1, \"petal_width\": 1.8}' docker logs api # what the server printed docker stop api docker rm api A Mac with Apple silicon (M1 and later) builds arm64 images. Most cloud servers run amd64. To build for them, add a platform. It runs slower on a Mac because Docker emulates the other processor. Terminal (macOS, Apple silicon)shell docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 -t iris-api:1.0.0-amd64 . Windows 10 and 11 Start Docker Desktop and check that it uses the WSL 2 engine (Settings, General). The whale icon in the taskbar must say Engine running. Run the commands in PowerShell. The Docker commands are the same as on macOS and Linux. The files that make_project.py writes use Unix line endings, which containers need. If you later add a shell script, keep it at LF in VS Code (bottom right corner), because a script saved with Windows CRLF endings fails inside a Linux container with a confusing not found error. PowerShell (Windows)shell python make_project.py cd iris-api docker build -t iris-api:1.0.0 . docker run -d --name api -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 Invoke-RestMethod http://127.0.0.1:8000/health Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post -Uri http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict -ContentType 'application/json' -Body '{\"sepal_length\": 5.9, \"sepal_width\": 3.0, \"petal_length\": 5.1, \"petal_width\": 1.8}' docker logs api # what the server printed docker stop api docker rm api Builds are faster when the project folder lives inside the WSL Linux file system (open Ubuntu from the Start menu and work in your Linux home folder) instead of on drive C, because Docker then reads the files without crossing between Windows and Linux. Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) Docker Engine runs as a system service. Add yourself to the docker group once so you do not need sudo before every command, then log out and back in. Terminal (Linux)shell sudo usermod -aG docker $USER # once, then log out and in sudo systemctl enable --now docker # Fedora: start the service (Ubuntu starts it already) python3 make_project.py # creates the folder iris-api cd iris-api docker build -t iris-api:1.0.0 . docker run -d --name api -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"sepal_length\": 5.9, \"sepal_width\": 3.0, \"petal_length\": 5.1, \"petal_width\": 1.8}' docker logs api # what the server printed docker stop api docker rm api Being in the"],[4,"build","Build and run the model container on your computer","docker group gives the same power as root on that machine, so only add trusted users. Rootless Docker is an option on shared machines. ChromeOS (Chromebook) Use Docker Engine inside the Linux development environment (installed in MLOps 1.1 with Docker’s Debian steps). An ARM Chromebook builds arm64 images, an Intel or AMD one builds amd64. Chrome opens http://localhost:8000/docs because ChromeOS forwards the port from Linux. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell sudo usermod -aG docker $USER # once, then restart the Linux environment python3 make_project.py # creates the folder iris-api cd iris-api docker build -t iris-api:1.0.0 . docker run -d --name api -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health docker logs api # what the server printed docker stop api docker rm api Building images uses a lot of disk space. If the build stops with no space left on device, raise the Linux disk size in Settings (search for Linux development environment, then Disk size) and run docker system prune to delete old layers. This is the real output of those commands for this project: Real run (Linux, Docker 29)shell $ docker build -t iris-api:1.0.0 . #8 [4/7] RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt #8 DONE 17.9s #10 [6/7] RUN python train.py #10 1.396 saved model/iris.joblib, test accuracy 0.9111 #12 naming to docker.io/library/iris-api:1.0.0 done $ docker images iris-api IMAGE ID DISK USAGE CONTENT SIZE iris-api:1.0.0 8ee36f3e2459 555MB 129MB $ curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health {\"status\":\"ok\",\"model_version\":\"1.0.0\"} $ curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict ... {\"species\":\"virginica\",\"probability\":0.653,\"model_version\":\"1.0.0\"} $ docker exec api whoami appuser $ docker ps --format \"{{.Names}} {{.Status}}\" api Up 34 seconds (healthy) Remember The probability here is 0.653, not the 0.703 of MLOps 2.2, because train.py holds back 30 percent of the flowers for testing and trains on the rest. The image takes 555 MB on disk but only 129 MB is downloaded, because layers are stored compressed. compose.yamlyaml 1services:2 api:3 build:4 context: .5 args:6 MODEL_VERSION: \"1.0.0\"7 image: iris-api:1.0.08 ports:9 - \"8000:8000\"10 restart: unless-stopped Line 2 One service called api. A bigger project adds a database or a monitoring service next to it. Line 4 Build from the Dockerfile in this folder. Line 6 Passed to the ARG line of the Dockerfile. Line 9 Computer port 8000 to container port 8000. Line 10 Start again after a crash or a reboot, unless you stopped it yourself. Terminal or PowerShell (every system)shell docker compose up -d --build # build if needed, then start in the background docker compose ps docker compose logs -f api # follow the logs, Control and C to stop following docker compose down # stop and remove Lesson 3 of 7"],[4,"tests","Testing a model, not only the code","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Breck, Cai, Nielsen, Salib and Sculley, The ML Test Score: A Rubric for ML Production Readiness (2017), and Ribeiro, Wu, Guestrin and Singh, Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP Models with CheckList (2020). What it is Normal tests check that code does what it should. A model also needs behaviour tests: a quality gate (accuracy must stay above a line), known examples that any good model gets right, invariance (tiny changes that should not change the answer) and directional checks (a change that should push the answer one way). Why we need it A training run can finish without any error and still produce a useless model, for example after a data bug shuffles the labels. Without behaviour tests, CI stays green and the bad model ships. How it works Write the tests with pytest next to the app. Keep them fast, so they run on every push. Choose the gate from the current model’s score and its uncertainty, not from a wish. Test Question it answers In this project API test Do the routes answer correctly? test_health, test_known_flower Input test Is bad input rejected? test_bad_input_is_rejected (422) Quality gate Is the model good enough to ship? test_accuracy_gate (at least 0.90) Invariance Does tiny noise leave the answer alone? test_small_noise_keeps_the_class Directional Does a change push the answer the right way? test_longer_petals_mean_virginica Latency Is it fast enough? test_latency_budget (under 50 ms) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 model_tests.py. Common mistake The broken model scored 0.2 and failed three of the four checks, so CI blocks it. Look closely: it still passed the invariance test, because a model that ignores its input is perfectly stable. One kind of test is never enough. tests/test_app.pypython 1# Tests that CI runs on every push: python -m pytest -q2import time34from fastapi.testclient import TestClient56from app import app, meta, model78client = TestClient(app)9SETOSA = {'sepal_length': 5.0, 'sepal_width': 3.4, 'petal_length': 1.5, 'petal_width': 0.2}101112def test_health():13 r = client.get('/health')14 assert r.status_code == 200 and r.json()['status'] == 'ok'151617def test_known_flower():18 r = client.post('/predict', json=SETOSA)19 assert r.status_code == 200 and r.json()['species'] == 'setosa'202122def test_bad_input_is_rejected():23 r = client.post('/predict', json={'sepal_length': -1})24 assert r.status_code == 422252627def test_accuracy_gate():28 assert meta['test_accuracy'] >= 0.90 # a worse model must not ship293031def test_small_noise_keeps_the_class():32 a = model.predict([[5.0, 3.4, 1.5, 0.2]])[0]33 b = model.predict([[5.02, 3.38, 1.51, 0.2]])[0]34 assert a == b353637def test_longer_petals_mean_virginica():38 p_small = model.predict_proba([[6.0, 3.0, 4.0, 1.2]])[0][2]39 p_large = model.predict_proba([[6.0, 3.0, 6.0, 2.2]])[0][2]40 assert p_large > p_small414243def test_latency_budget():44 start = time.perf_counter()45 for _ in range(100):46 client.post('/predict', json=SETOSA)47 assert (time.perf_counter() - start) / 100 < 0.05 # under 50 ms per request Line 4 TestClient calls the app in the same process, no server needed. Line 6 Importing app also loads the model, exactly as the server does. Line 9 A flower that every good model calls setosa. Line 28 The quality gate. A model below 0.90 fails CI and cannot be merged. Line 31 Invariance: a change of a few hundredths of a centimetre must not change the class. Line 37 Directional: longer, wider petals should make virginica more likely. Line 43 A rough latency budget. Real load tests use tools such as Locust on the running container. Chance a suite of flaky tests is greenExam favourite P(green) = (1 − p)n n independent tests that each fail at random with probability p. Ten tests at 2 percent: 0.9810 = 0.817. Uncertainty of a test accuracyExam favourite SE = √(p(1 − p) / n) With p = 0.9111 on n = 45 test rows, SE = 0.042,"],[4,"tests","Testing a model, not only the code","so a gate at 0.90 is less than one standard error away. Lesson 4 of 7"],[4,"ci","CI/CD with GitHub Actions","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: GitHub Actions documentation: Workflow syntax, Building and testing Python, and Publishing Docker images. Humble and Farley, Continuous Delivery (2010). What it is Continuous integration (CI) runs the same checks on a clean machine for every push and pull request. Continuous delivery (CD) turns every green change on main into a released artefact, here a container image in a registry, ready to deploy. GitHub Actions runs both from a YAML file in .github/workflows. Why we need it Checks that only run on your laptop get skipped on a busy day, and a laptop has packages a clean server does not. CI catches a broken model or a missing file before anyone merges it, and CD makes every release the same few steps. How it works Commit the workflow file. Make the pull request wait for the green check (branch protection). Tag each image with the commit id, keep passwords in repository secrets, and roll back by running the previous tag. .github/workflows/ci.ymlyaml 1name: model-ci23on:4 push:5 branches: [main]6 pull_request:78jobs:9 test:10 runs-on: ubuntu-latest11 steps:12 - uses: actions/checkout@v413 - uses: actions/setup-python@v514 with:15 python-version: \"3.14\"16 cache: pip17 - run: pip install -r requirements-dev.txt18 - run: python train.py19 - run: python -m pytest -q2021 image:22 needs: test23 runs-on: ubuntu-latest24 permissions:25 contents: read26 packages: write27 steps:28 - uses: actions/checkout@v429 - name: Build the image30 run: docker build --build-arg MODEL_VERSION=${{ github.sha }} -t iris-api:ci .31 - name: Smoke test the container32 run: |33 docker run -d --name api -p 8000:8000 iris-api:ci34 for i in $(seq 1 20); do curl -fs http://127.0.0.1:8000/health && break || sleep 1; done35 curl -fs -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\36 -d '{\"sepal_length\": 5.0, \"sepal_width\": 3.4, \"petal_length\": 1.5, \"petal_width\": 0.2}'37 - name: Push to GitHub Container Registry38 if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'39 run: |40 echo \"${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\" | docker login ghcr.io -u ${{ github.actor }} --password-stdin41 IMAGE=ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}42 docker tag iris-api:ci $IMAGE43 docker push $IMAGE Line 3 When to run: every push to main and every pull request. Line 9 The first job installs, trains and tests. Line 10 A fresh Ubuntu machine for every run. Line 16 Cache downloaded packages between runs to save time. Line 17 The same pinned versions as on your computer. Line 19 Any failing test turns the run red. Line 22 The image job only starts when the test job passed. Line 26 Allows this job to push to GitHub Container Registry with the built in token. Line 30 The commit id becomes the model version inside the image. Line 34 Wait up to 20 seconds for the container to answer /health. Line 35 curl -f fails on any 4xx or 5xx status, so a broken /predict also turns the run red. Line 38 Only main publishes an image. Pull requests stop after the smoke test. Line 40 GITHUB_TOKEN is created for every run. Never write a password in the file itself. Line 41 Image names in ghcr.io must be lower case, so use a lower case repository name. Run exactly what CI runs on your own computer before you push. This is the real result for this project: 7 passed in 1.57s. Run the CI checks on your computer macOS Terminal (macOS)shell cd iris-api python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate pip install -r requirements-dev.txt python train.py python -m pytest -q Windows 10 and 11 PowerShell (Windows)shell cd iris-api py -3.14 -m venv .venv .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 pip install -r requirements-dev.txt python train.py python -m pytest -q Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) Terminal (Linux)shell cd iris-api python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate pip install -r requirements-dev.txt python train.py python -m pytest -q ChromeOS (Chromebook) Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell cd iris-api uv venv --python 3.14 && source"],[4,"ci","CI/CD with GitHub Actions",".venv/bin/activate uv pip install -r requirements-dev.txt python train.py python -m pytest -q Turn it on for your repository Create a repository on GitHub and push the iris-api folder, including .github/workflows/ci.yml. Open the Actions tab. The workflow named model-ci starts on its own for the push. In Settings, Branches, add a rule for main that requires the test and image checks to pass before merging. Open a pull request that lowers the gate or breaks the model, and watch the check turn red. After a merge to main, find the new image under Packages on the repository page. Going deeper: Continuous training, canary releases and rollbacks CI/CD for ML often adds a third loop, continuous training: a schedule or a drift alarm retrains the model on fresh data, and the new model goes through the same gate. Releases are safer as a canary: send 5 percent of traffic to the new image, compare its errors with the old one, and only then move everyone over. Because every image is tagged with its commit id, a rollback is one command that runs the previous tag. Lesson 5 of 7"],[4,"versus","Virtual environment, container or virtual machine","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Ways to package a model service Virtual environment Docker container Virtual machine Pins Python packages only Packages, Python, system libraries and OS files A whole operating system and kernel Start time Instant About a second Tens of seconds Size Hundreds of MB Hundreds of MB, shared layers Many GB Same on every computer No: OS and Python can differ Yes, for the same processor type Yes Isolation None Process level, shares the host kernel Strong, own kernel Best for Writing and testing code Shipping services, CI, Kubernetes Strict isolation or a different OS Bottom line: Develop in a virtual environment, ship in a container, and leave virtual machines to the platform that runs your containers. Lesson 6 of 7"],[4,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths Pack the model and everything it needs into one box that runs the same on any computer, and let a robot test every change before it goes out. Write a Dockerfile with good layer order, run it with Compose, test the model with a quality gate and behaviour tests, and run them in GitHub Actions. Build multi stage and multi platform images, publish them to a registry tagged by commit, protect branches, and choose gates from the uncertainty of the score. Run continuous training with canary releases and automatic rollback, sign images and scan them for vulnerabilities, and deploy to Kubernetes with GitOps. Beginner Build and run the model container. Intermediate Test and ship every change automatically. Expert Release models safely at scale. Lesson 7 of 7"],[4,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. An image packs the OS layer, Python, the pinned packages, the code and the model. A container is a running copy. The same image runs the same on every system. Order the Dockerfile for the layer cache: requirements and pip install first, code later. A code change then rebuilt in 2.2 s instead of 32 s. Run as a user that is not root, add a HEALTHCHECK, and start with docker run -p 8000:8000 or docker compose up. Test the model, not only the code: a quality gate, known examples, invariance and directional checks. The broken model still passed invariance, so use several kinds. GitHub Actions runs install, train, test, build, smoke test and push on every change, and the first failure blocks the release. Next: Coming next: tracking experiments and keeping a registry of model versions. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: bump a model version, check a quality gate, and work out which Docker layers rebuild.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[5,"","Experiment tracking and the model registry","Tracking runs, MLflow with per OS steps, the winner's curse, and a model registry with promotion and rollback. · experiment tracking · MLflow · model registry · model alias · champion challenger · selection bias · random search · rollback · lineage · reproducibility Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 3: Operate and improve›Tracking and registry Step 3 · Topic 1 of 3 · Advanced Record every training run with its parameters, metrics, data and code versions, run MLflow on your own computer, choose the champion without fooling yourself, and manage releases with a model registry of versions and aliases. About 50 min7 lessons2 animations2 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Experiment tracking: a record of every run 2MLflow on your own computer 3Choosing the best run without fooling yourself 4The model registry: versions, aliases and promotion 5Tracking tools compared 6From beginner to expert 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[5,"track","Experiment tracking: a record of every run","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: MLflow documentation, Tracking. Sculley and colleagues, Hidden technical debt in machine learning systems (2015). What it is An experiment tracker stores, for every training run, the parameters (settings such as C), the metrics (such as validation AUC), the artifacts (the model file, plots), and the context: code version, data fingerprint, time and who ran it. Runs are grouped into experiments and can be searched, sorted and compared. Why we need it After a week of trying ideas nobody remembers which settings gave the good score, which data it used, or where the model file went. A spreadsheet of results drifts away from the real runs. A tracker records everything automatically, so any result can be found, compared and reproduced. How it works Wrap each training run in a tracked run, log parameters before training and metrics after, save the model as an artifact, and tag the data version and the Git commit. Compare runs only when they used the same data and the same validation split. Python # A tiny experiment tracker in plain Python, to see what MLflow and similar tools store for every run:# parameters, metrics, a fingerprint of the data, the code version and the time.import hashlibimport jsonimport timeimport numpy as npfrom sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancerfrom sklearn.linear_model importLogisticRegressionfrom sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_scorefrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_splitfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipelinefrom sklearn.preprocessing importStandardScalerLOG = 'runs.jsonl'# one JSON line per runopen(LOG, 'w').close()def log_run(params, metrics, data_hash, code_version): run = {'id': len(open(LOG).readlines()) + 1, 'time': time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M'),'params': params, 'metrics': metrics, 'data': data_hash, 'code': code_version}with open(LOG, 'a') as f: f.write(json.dumps(run) + '\\n')X, y = load_breast_cancer(return_X_y=True)X_train, X_val, y_train, y_val = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.25, random_state=0, stratify=y)data_hash = hashlib.sha256(X_train.tobytes() + y_train.tobytes()).hexdigest()[:12]forCin [0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0]:for weight in [None, 'balanced']: model = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression(C=C, class_weight=weight, max_iter=2000)).fit(X_train, y_train) auc = roc_auc_score(y_val, model.predict_proba(X_val)[:, 1]) log_run({'C': C, 'class_weight': weight}, {'val_auc': round(auc, 4)}, data_hash, 'git:3f2a9c1')runs = [json.loads(line) for line in open(LOG)]print(len(runs), 'runs logged. One of them, exactly as stored:')print(' ', json.dumps(runs[0]))best = max(runs, key=lambda r: r['metrics']['val_auc'])print('Best run:', best['id'], best['params'], best['metrics'])same_data = all(r['data'] == data_hash for r in runs)print('All runs used the same data:', same_data, '| so their scores can be compared fairly') Output 8 runs logged. One of them, exactly as stored: {\"id\": 1, \"time\": \"2026-11-14 09:44\", \"params\": {\"C\": 0.01, \"class_weight\": null}, \"metrics\": {\"val_auc\": 0.9849}, \"data\": \"cc082771e3fa\", \"code\": \"git:3f2a9c1\"} Best run: 5 {'C': 1.0, 'class_weight': None} {'val_auc': 0.9952} All runs used the same data: True | so their scores can be compared fairly Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 tracker_lite.py. Remember Each line of runs.jsonl is one complete record: settings, score, a 12 character fingerprint of the training data and the Git commit. Because every run has the same data fingerprint, their scores are comparable. MLflow stores the same facts in a database instead of a text file. What to log Example Why Parameters C = 1.0, class_weight = None To know what produced the result Metrics val_auc = 0.9952 To compare and rank runs Artifacts model file, confusion matrix plot To reuse the exact model later Data version a hash of the training data, or a DVC tag Scores on different data cannot be compared Code version Git commit id To"],[5,"track","Experiment tracking: a record of every run","rerun exactly the same code Environment Python and package versions The same code can behave differently with other versions Lesson 2 of 7"],[5,"mlflow","MLflow on your own computer","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: MLflow 3 documentation: Tracking quickstart, mlflow.search_runs, mlflow.sklearn.log_model, Model Registry and model aliases. What it is MLflow is an open source platform for tracking runs, packaging models and keeping a model registry. Runs go into a backend store (here a SQLite file) and model files into an artifact store (here the folder mlruns). mlflow ui shows them in the browser. Why we need it It is free, works offline on one laptop, and grows into a shared team server without changing your training code. Many cloud platforms (Databricks, Azure ML) speak the same MLflow API. How it works Point MLflow at a store, choose an experiment, and inside with mlflow.start_run() log parameters, metrics and the model. Then search the runs, register the best model and give it an alias. Run this one on your own computer. It needs the mlflow package and writes a database and model files, which the browser version of Python cannot install or keep. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 mlflow_track.py. In the exam Four runs were logged and sorted by validation AUC. C = 1.0 won with 0.9952, was registered as version 1 of breast-cancer-classifier with the alias champion, and loading models:/breast-cancer-classifier@champion gave back exactly the same AUC. Install MLflow, run the program and open the MLflow page macOS Activate your project environment and install MLflow. It is a big package, so the install takes a minute. Run the program, then start the MLflow web page on port 5001. On macOS 12 and later port 5000, the MLflow default, is taken by the AirPlay Receiver and answers 403. Open http://127.0.0.1:5001, click the breast-cancer experiment, tick the four runs and press Compare. Terminal (macOS)shell source .venv/bin/activate pip install mlflow scikit-learn python mlflow_track.py mlflow ui --backend-store-uri sqlite:///mlflow.db --port 5001 Windows 10 and 11 Activate the environment in PowerShell and install MLflow. If PowerShell says the word mlflow is not recognised, the Scripts folder is not on the path. python -m mlflow always works. Open http://127.0.0.1:5000. If Windows Defender Firewall asks, allow private networks only. PowerShell (Windows)shell .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 pip install mlflow scikit-learn python mlflow_track.py python -m mlflow ui --backend-store-uri sqlite:///mlflow.db --port 5000 Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) The same as macOS, and port 5000 is usually free. On a shared server add --host 0.0.0.0 only behind a login or a VPN, because the MLflow page has no password of its own. Terminal (Linux)shell source .venv/bin/activate pip install mlflow scikit-learn python mlflow_track.py mlflow ui --backend-store-uri sqlite:///mlflow.db --port 5000 ChromeOS (Chromebook) Run it in the Linux development environment and open http://localhost:5000 in Chrome, which ChromeOS forwards from Linux. MLflow pulls in many packages, so allow about 1 GB of free Linux disk space. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell source .venv/bin/activate uv pip install mlflow scikit-learn python mlflow_track.py mlflow ui --backend-store-uri sqlite:///mlflow.db --port 5000 Going deeper: From one laptop to a team server A team runs one tracking server with a real database and shared artifact storage, for example mlflow server --backend-store-uri postgresql://... --artifacts-destination s3://bucket/mlflow --host 0.0.0.0, behind a login. Everyone sets MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI to its address and the training code stays the same. The MLflow project also publishes an official Docker image (ghcr.io/mlflow/mlflow) for running that server in a container (MLOps 2.3). Lesson 3 of 7"],[5,"curse","Choosing the best run without fooling yourself","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Cawley and Talbot, On over-fitting in model selection and subsequent selection bias in performance evaluation (2010), and Bergstra and Bengio, Random search for hyper-parameter optimization (2012). What it is When you compare many runs on one validation set and keep the best, part of its lead is luck. This selection bias, also called the winner’s curse, makes the champion’s validation score too optimistic. Why we need it Trackers make it easy to try hundreds of runs. The more you try, the larger the bias, until a model that learned nothing can look good. How it works Keep a test set that no run ever sees and score only the final champion on it, once. Use cross validation for the comparison, prefer simpler settings when scores are within noise, and report how many runs you tried. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 winners_curse.py. Common mistake The labels are coin flips, so every model is truly 50 percent accurate. The best of 1000 runs still showed 0.615 on validation, then 0.530 on fresh test data. With 200 validation rows one standard error is 0.035, and the best of 1000 lands about three standard errors above the truth. Random search coverageExam favourite P(at least one of n runs in the top q) = 1 − (1 − q)n 60 random runs reach the top 5 percent with 95 percent chance. Expected best of n noisy scoresExam favourite about σ √(2 ln n) above the mean With σ = 0.02 and n = 100: 0.061, close to the 0.064 drop in the animation. Lesson 4 of 7"],[5,"registry","The model registry: versions, aliases and promotion","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: MLflow documentation, Model Registry and model aliases. Google, MLOps: Continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine learning. What it is A model registry keeps every approved model as a numbered, unchangeable version with its lineage (which run, data and code made it). Aliases such as champion and challenger are movable labels, and services load a model by alias, for example models:/breast-cancer-classifier@champion. Why we need it Without a registry, the model in production is a file somebody copied, and nobody can say which run made it or how to go back. With versions and aliases, promotion and rollback are one recorded action, and the service code never changes. How it works Register candidates as new versions, evaluate a challenger against the champion on the same held out data, promote only past a margin and after review, and roll back by moving the alias. Never edit a version, register a new one. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 registry_lite.py. In the exam v2 (AUC 0.8844) was kept but not promoted. v3 (0.9942) beat v1 (0.9555) and became champion. After the incident, champion moved back to v1 in one line, with no retraining and no rebuild. A promotion checklist The challenger was trained by the pipeline from tracked code and data, not by hand. It beats the champion on the same held out data by more than the noise (the gate). It passes the behaviour tests of MLOps 2.3 and a fairness check by group (MLOps 3.3). Latency and memory fit the service. A person approves, the alias moves, and the change is logged with the reason. Lesson 5 of 7"],[5,"versus","Tracking tools compared","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Experiment tracking tools MLflow Weights and Biases DVC TensorBoard Type Open source platform Hosted service (also self hosted) Open source, Git based Open source viewer Strength Tracking, registry and model packaging in one Rich dashboards and team features Versions data and pipelines next to Git Training curves for deep learning Model registry Yes Yes Through Git tags and its studio No Works offline Yes Mostly needs its server Yes Yes Best for A free, standard stack Teams who want polish and hosting Data versioning and reproducible pipelines Watching one training run Bottom line: MLflow is the common free default, DVC pairs well with it for data versions, and hosted tools trade money for convenience. Lesson 6 of 7"],[5,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths Write down every try: the settings, the score and where the model is saved, so you can always find the best one again. Log parameters, metrics, artifacts, data and code versions with MLflow, compare runs fairly, and register the champion with an alias. Guard against selection bias with an untouched test set, gate promotions on held out data, and run a shared tracking server with lineage. Automate promotion in pipelines with approvals and audit logs, track data lineage end to end, and design registries that serve many models across teams. Beginner Keep a record of every run. Intermediate Choose and register the champion fairly. Expert Automate safe promotion and rollback. Lesson 7 of 7"],[5,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. An experiment tracker stores parameters, metrics, artifacts, data fingerprint and code version for every run. MLflow logs runs inside mlflow.start_run(), searches them with search_runs, and shows them with mlflow ui (port 5001 on macOS because of AirPlay). The best of many runs on one validation set is too optimistic: 0.615 against 0.530 for 1000 coin flip models. Keep an untouched test set. A model registry keeps frozen versions and movable aliases. Services load models:/name@champion. Promote a challenger only past a gate on the same held out data, and roll back by moving the alias. Next: Coming next: watching a model in production for drift and decay. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: pick the best run, decide a promotion, and work out how many random runs a search needs.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[6,"","Monitoring and drift","What to monitor, PSI and KS drift tests, concept against data drift, retraining, and a scheduled drift job on every OS. · model monitoring · data drift · concept drift · PSI · KS test · SLO · p99 latency · retraining · cron · Task Scheduler Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 3: Operate and improve›Monitoring and drift Step 3 · Topic 2 of 3 · Advanced Watch a live model on four layers, measure tail latency against an SLO, detect data drift with PSI and the KS test, catch concept drift through accuracy, schedule a daily drift job on every operating system, and respond to alerts with a playbook. About 55 min7 lessons2 animations6 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What to watch once a model is live 2Drift: when live data stops looking like training data 3Concept drift, label delay and retraining 4Run a drift check every day on your computer 5When an alert fires 6From beginner to expert 7Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 7"],[6,"what","What to watch once a model is live","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Google, Site Reliability Engineering (2016), chapters on service level objectives, and Huyen, Designing Machine Learning Systems (2022) chapter 8. What it is Monitoring means measuring a live model all the time on four layers: the service (latency, errors, traffic), the data (missing values, schema, drift), the model (prediction distribution, accuracy once labels arrive) and the business (the metric the model exists to move). Why we need it A model can fail silently. The API keeps answering 200 while the data underneath has changed and the predictions have become useless. Normal software monitoring sees nothing wrong, so ML needs extra checks. How it works Set a target for each layer, such as 99 percent of requests under 200 ms (a service level objective, SLO). Collect the numbers from every request, look at percentiles rather than averages, and alert only on sustained problems that need a person. Layer Examples Needs true labels? Service p50, p95, p99 latency, error rate, requests per second No Data missing share, unseen categories, out of range values, PSI per feature No Model prediction mean and spread, PSI of the scores, accuracy, calibration Accuracy needs labels Business fraud losses, click through rate, churn Usually, and with delay Python # Service health of a model API for one day: latency percentiles and error rate against an SLO.import numpy as nprng = np.random.default_rng(3)n = 100_000# requests in one daylatency = rng.lognormal(mean=np.log(18), sigma=0.35, size=n) # most requests take about 18 msslow = rng.random(n) < 0.02# 2 percent hit a cold cache or a long inputlatency[slow] += rng.uniform(150, 400, slow.sum())errors = rng.random(n) < 0.0007# 500 errorsp50, p95, p99 = np.percentile(latency, [50, 95, 99])print(f'mean {latency.mean():.1f} ms p50 {p50:.1f} ms p95 {p95:.1f} ms p99 {p99:.1f} ms')print('The mean looks fine, but 1 request in 100 waits more than', round(p99), 'ms.')slo_latency, slo_success = 200, 0.999# 99 percent under 200 ms, 99.9 percent succeedprint(f'Share under {slo_latency} ms: {np.mean(latency < slo_latency):.4f} (target 0.99)')budget = (1 - slo_success) * n # errors the SLO allows todayprint(f'Errors: {errors.sum()} of an error budget of {budget:.0f}, so {errors.sum() / budget:.0%} of the budget is used') Output mean 24.5 ms p50 18.2 ms p95 34.4 ms p99 291.6 ms The mean looks fine, but 1 request in 100 waits more than 292 ms. Share under 200 ms: 0.9827 (target 0.99) Errors: 65 of an error budget of 100, so 65% of the budget is used Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 service_metrics.py. Common mistake The average latency is 24.5 ms, which sounds fine, but the 99th percentile is 292 ms, so 1 request in 100 is ten times slower than typical. Only 98.27 percent of requests met the 200 ms target, below the 99 percent SLO, while errors used 65 percent of the day’s budget. PercentileExam favourite p99 = the value that 99 percent of requests stay below Tail latency is what users notice. Error budgetExam favourite allowed failures = (1 − SLO) × requests 99.9 percent of 100,000 leaves a budget of 100 errors. Burn rate actual error rate / allowed error rate A burn rate of 5 uses a 30 day budget in 6 days. Lesson 2 of 7"],[6,"drift","Drift: when live data stops looking like training data","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Rabanser, Guennemann and Lipton, Failing loudly (2019), Siddiqi, Credit risk scorecards (2006), and SciPy, scipy.stats.ks_2samp. What it is Data drift (covariate shift) means the inputs change, for example younger customers after a marketing campaign. Label shift means the share of each class changes. Concept drift means the link between inputs and label changes, for example fraudsters change tactics. Drift tests compare a live window with the training data. Why we need it A model only knows the world it was trained on. Drift is the early warning that its answers may be getting worse, often weeks before enough true labels arrive to measure accuracy. How it works For each numeric feature compute the PSI (size of the change, thresholds 0.1 and 0.25) and the KS test (is there any change at all). For categories use a chi square test and watch for unseen values. Track the model’s own score distribution too. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 drift_tests.py. In the exam A shift of 8 in the mean gives PSI 0.610, clear drift. A wider spread gives 0.246 with no change in the mean, which a check on averages would miss. With 200,000 rows a harmless shift of 0.3 already has a KS p value of 1.3e−12, while PSI is 0.001. Use the size of the change to decide, and the p value only as a hint. Population stability indexExam favourite PSI = Σbins (qi − pi) ln(qi / pi) p training shares, q live shares. Below 0.1 stable, 0.1 to 0.25 watch, above 0.25 drift. Kolmogorov Smirnov statisticExam favourite D = maxx |Ftrain(x) − Flive(x)| Largest gap between the two cumulative curves. PSI and KL divergence PSI = KL(q || p) + KL(p || q) For two normals with the same spread: (Δμ / σ)². Lesson 3 of 7"],[6,"decay","Concept drift, label delay and retraining","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Gama and colleagues, A survey on concept drift adaptation (2014). What it is Input checks cannot see concept drift, because the inputs look the same. It only shows in the model’s accuracy, which needs true labels, and labels often arrive late: a loan default shows up months later, a churn label after the customer leaves. Why we need it Teams that only watch input drift miss the most damaging failure, and teams that retrain on every drift alarm waste effort on changes that did no harm. How it works Collect labels wherever you can (user feedback, manual review of a sample, later outcomes). Watch proxies while waiting, such as the score distribution. Retrain on a schedule, or when performance drops past a threshold, always through the tested pipeline and the registry gate of MLOps 3.1. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 decay_sim.py. In the exam Under concept drift accuracy fell from 0.95 to 0.57 in a year while the PSI of the input stayed at 0.01. Retraining every 3 months kept it between 0.86 and 0.94. Under data drift the PSI reached 6.20 but accuracy stayed at 0.94 to 0.95, because the model’s rule still held. Retraining strategy When it fits Cost On a schedule (weekly, monthly) Steady, slow drift and labels arrive regularly Simple, may retrain without need On a trigger (accuracy or drift past a threshold) Irregular changes Needs reliable monitoring Online or continual learning Fast changing data such as ads or news Complex, risk of learning from bad data Lesson 4 of 7"],[6,"schedule","Run a drift check every day on your computer","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: crontab(5), Apple launchd documentation, Microsoft schtasks documentation, and GitHub Actions, Events that trigger workflows (schedule). What it is A scheduled job runs the drift check by itself every day, writes a report, and exits with an error code when something drifted, so the scheduler or CI can alert you. Why we need it A check that you must remember to run is a check that stops running. A schedule makes monitoring automatic. How it works Download the job, run it once by hand, then register it with the scheduler of your system below, or with a GitHub Actions schedule that works the same for everyone. Run this one on your own computer. It is meant to be started every day by your computer’s scheduler, which a browser page cannot do. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 daily_drift_job.py. Remember On the demo data the income column moved (PSI 0.472) and 6 percent of ages went missing, above the 5 percent limit, so both are flagged. The score column needs watching (0.155). With --strict the job exits with code 1, which schedulers and CI treat as a failure. Schedule the job every morning at 06:00 macOS macOS has two schedulers. cron is simple and the same as on Linux. launchd is Apple’s own, and unlike cron it runs a job that was missed while the Mac was asleep once it wakes up. Run the job once by hand and check that drift_report.json appears. Open your crontab and add the line below (use your own folder). The five fields are minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week. If the project lives in Documents, Desktop or Downloads, macOS privacy protection blocks cron. Give /usr/sbin/cron Full Disk Access in System Settings, Privacy and Security, or keep the project in another folder such as ~/ml-project. Terminal (macOS)shell cd ~/ml-project && .venv/bin/python daily_drift_job.py crontab -e # add this line, save and close: 0 6 * * * ~/ml-project/.venv/bin/python ~/ml-project/daily_drift_job.py --strict >> ~/ml-project/drift.log 2>&1 crontab -l # check it is there Windows 10 and 11 Windows uses Task Scheduler. The schtasks command creates a task without clicking through the Task Scheduler window. The job changes to its own folder first, so the task can start anywhere. PowerShell (Windows)shell cd $HOME\\ml-project .venv\\Scripts\\python.exe daily_drift_job.py # once by hand schtasks /Create /SC DAILY /ST 06:00 /TN \"DriftCheck\" /TR \"$HOME\\ml-project\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe $HOME\\ml-project\\daily_drift_job.py --strict\" schtasks /Run /TN \"DriftCheck\" # run it now to test schtasks /Query /TN \"DriftCheck\" /V /FO LIST # Last Result 0 means no drift, 1 means drift The task only runs while you are signed in, unless you open it in Task Scheduler and choose Run whether user is logged on or not. A laptop that is off at 06:00 skips the run unless you also tick Run task as soon as possible after a scheduled start is missed. Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) Use cron, or a systemd timer on servers. With Persistent=true a timer also catches up on runs missed while the machine was off. Terminal (Linux)shell cd ~/ml-project && .venv/bin/python daily_drift_job.py crontab -e 0 6 * * * ~/ml-project/.venv/bin/python ~/ml-project/daily_drift_job.py --strict >> ~/ml-project/drift.log 2>&1 grep CRON /var/log/syslog | tail # Ubuntu and Debian: did it run journalctl -u cron --since today # or ask systemd (the service is crond on Fedora) ChromeOS (Chromebook) The Linux environment on a Chromebook only runs while it is open, so a cron job there misses runs while the Chromebook sleeps. Use it to test the job, and run the real schedule in GitHub Actions (below), which works the same whatever computer you use. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell sudo apt install cron # cron is not installed by default sudo service cron start crontab -e 0 6 * * * ~/ml-project/.venv/bin/python"],[6,"schedule","Run a drift check every day on your computer","~/ml-project/daily_drift_job.py --strict >> ~/ml-project/drift.log 2>&1 .github/workflows/drift.ymlyaml 1name: daily-drift-check2on:3 schedule:4 - cron: \"0 6 * * *\" # every day at 06:00 UTC5 workflow_dispatch: # plus a Run workflow button6jobs:7 drift:8 runs-on: ubuntu-latest9 steps:10 - uses: actions/checkout@v411 - uses: actions/setup-python@v512 with:13 python-version: \"3.14\"14 - run: pip install numpy pandas15 - run: python daily_drift_job.py --strict16 - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v417 if: always()18 with:19 name: drift-report20 path: drift_report.json Line 4 Cron syntax in UTC: minute 0, hour 6, every day. Line 5 Adds a button to run it by hand from the Actions tab. Line 15 --strict makes the run fail, and GitHub emails you, when anything drifted. Line 17 Keep the report even when the run failed. Lesson 5 of 7"],[6,"respond","When an alert fires","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. A drift and decay playbook Check the pipeline first: a broken join, a renamed column or a unit change causes most sudden drift. Measure the impact: did accuracy, calibration or the business metric move, or only an input? If the model is wrong and a previous version was fine, roll back the champion alias (MLOps 3.1). If the world changed, retrain on recent labelled data through the tested pipeline and the promotion gate. Write down the cause and add a test or a monitor so the same failure is caught earlier next time. Drift checks compared PSI KS test Chi square Wasserstein distance Data type Numeric (binned) or categories Numeric Categories Numeric Answers How big is the change Is there any change Is there any change How far did the values move Sensitive to sample size Little Very: big samples flag tiny changes Very Little Rule of thumb 0.1 watch, 0.25 drift p below 0.05, used with care p below 0.05, used with care Compare with the feature’s spread Bottom line: Use PSI or Wasserstein distance to decide and alert, and the tests as a second opinion. Tools such as Evidently compute all of them, and Prometheus with Grafana stores and charts the service metrics. Lesson 6 of 7"],[6,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths Keep checking that the model is still fast, still answering and still seeing the kind of data it learned from. Track latency percentiles and error budgets, compute PSI and KS per feature, and schedule a daily drift report with alerts. Tell data, label and concept drift apart, handle label delay with proxies and sampled labels, and choose scheduled or triggered retraining. Detect drift in high dimensional data such as text and images with embeddings and two sample tests, adapt models online, and estimate accuracy without labels. Beginner Watch a live model every day. Intermediate Detect drift and respond with a playbook. Expert Keep models accurate as the world changes. Lesson 7 of 7"],[6,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Monitor four layers: service (p99 latency, errors), data, model and business. The mean latency hid a 292 ms p99. PSI measures how much a feature moved (0.1 watch, 0.25 drift). KS flags any change, and with huge samples it flags harmless ones. Concept drift leaves inputs alone and destroyed accuracy (0.95 to 0.57). Data drift raised PSI to 6.2 with no loss of accuracy. Schedule the drift job with cron or launchd (macOS), Task Scheduler (Windows), cron or systemd (Linux), or GitHub Actions on any system. On an alert: check the pipeline, measure impact, roll back or retrain through the gate, then add a test. Next: Coming next: explaining predictions and checking that a model is fair to every group. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: PSI from bin shares, a nearest rank percentile, and an alert that needs several bad days in a row.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[7,"","Explainability and fairness","Permutation importance, Shapley values and SHAP, fairness metrics by group, mitigation, impossibility results and model cards. · explainability · SHAP · Shapley values · permutation importance · fairness · disparate impact · equal opportunity · Fairlearn · model card Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 3: Operate and improve›Explainability and fairness Step 3 · Topic 3 of 3 · Advanced Find which features a model really uses with permutation importance, explain single predictions with Shapley values and SHAP, audit a model for fairness by group, fix a gap with group thresholds, install SHAP and Fairlearn on every operating system, and document the result in a model card. About 55 min8 lessons2 animations9 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Which features does the model use? 2Shapley values: why this one prediction? 3Is the model fair to every group? 4SHAP and Fairlearn on your own computer 5Fixing unfairness, and why you cannot have every kind 6Model cards and responsible release 7From beginner to expert 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[7,"global","Which features does the model use?","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Breiman, Random forests (2001), Strobl and colleagues, Bias in random forest variable importance measures (2007), and scikit-learn, Permutation feature importance. What it is Global importance ranks the features by how much the whole model relies on them. Impurity importance (feature_importances_ in a tree model) adds up how much each feature reduced impurity in the training splits. Permutation importance shuffles one column of held out data and measures how much the score drops. Why we need it Teams, auditors and regulators ask what drives a model. Importance also finds bugs: a model that leans on an ID column or a leaked target is wrong, however good its score looks. How it works Prefer permutation importance on test data. Shuffle each feature several times, report the mean drop and its spread, and treat two strongly correlated features with care, because shuffling one leaves the other to carry the same information. Python # Which features does the model really use? Impurity importance against permutation importance.# We add a column of random ID numbers that has nothing to do with the label.import numpy as npfrom sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancerfrom sklearn.ensemble importRandomForestClassifierfrom sklearn.inspection import permutation_importancefrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_splitdata = load_breast_cancer()keep = ['worst radius', 'worst concave points', 'mean texture', 'worst smoothness']X = data.data[:, [list(data.feature_names).index(k) for k in keep]]rng = np.random.default_rng(0)X = np.column_stack([X, rng.integers(0, 100000, len(X))]) # random_id: pure noise, many valuesnames = keep + ['random_id']X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, data.target, test_size=0.3, random_state=0, stratify=data.target)forest = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=300, random_state=0).fit(X_train, y_train)print('test accuracy', round(forest.score(X_test, y_test), 3))perm = permutation_importance(forest, X_test, y_test, n_repeats=20, random_state=0)print(f'{\"feature\":22s} {\"impurity\":>9s} {\"permutation (test)\":>19s}')for i in np.argsort(-perm.importances_mean):print(f'{names[i]:22s} {forest.feature_importances_[i]:9.3f} {perm.importances_mean[i]:12.3f} +- {perm.importances_std[i]:.3f}')print('Impurity importance gives the random ID a share because it offers many split points on training data.')print('Permutation importance on test data shows that shuffling it changes nothing.') Output test accuracy 0.936 feature impurity permutation (test) worst radius 0.442 0.170 +- 0.028 worst concave points 0.398 0.103 +- 0.021 mean texture 0.082 0.010 +- 0.008 worst smoothness 0.060 0.008 +- 0.007 random_id 0.018 -0.008 +- 0.006 Impurity importance gives the random ID a share because it offers many split points on training data. Permutation importance on test data shows that shuffling it changes nothing. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 importance.py. In the exam Impurity importance always adds up to 1 and gives worst radius 0.442. On test data, shuffling worst radius costs 0.170 accuracy, the real dependence. The random ID column, pure noise, still got 0.018 of the impurity credit because its many distinct values offer many split points on training data. Shuffling it changed the test score by −0.008, which is just noise. Permutation importanceExam favourite Ij = s − (1/K) Σk sk,j s is the test score, sk,j the score after the k-th shuffle of feature j. Impurity importance Ij = Σsplits on j (nnode/n) Δimpurity Measured on training data, biased towards features with many values. Method Data it uses Strength Weakness Impurity (tree models) Training Free, instant Favours many valued features, says nothing about test data Permutation Test Any model, measures real dependence Correlated features share or hide credit Mean |SHAP| Any Consistent with local explanations Slower for non tree models"],[7,"global","Which features does the model use?","Lesson 2 of 8"],[7,"shap","Shapley values: why this one prediction?","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Shapley, A value for n-person games (1953), and Lundberg and Lee, A unified approach to interpreting model predictions (2017). What it is A Shapley value splits one prediction among the features. Start from the base value, the average prediction. Add the features one at a time and record what each adds. Average that over every order of adding them. SHAP is the family of fast methods that compute or estimate these values for real models. Why we need it A loan applicant who is refused is owed a reason, and a data scientist debugging one strange prediction needs to see which inputs pushed it. Shapley values are the only way of splitting a prediction that is fair in a precise sense: contributions add up exactly, identical features get equal credit and an unused feature gets zero. How it works For a few features, try every order, as the program does. For tree models use SHAP’s TreeExplainer, which is exact and fast. For any other model use a sampling explainer with a background set of a few hundred typical rows. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 shapley.py. In the exam The base value is 0.450, the average predicted chance of repaying. This applicant’s debt ratio of 0.82 pulls the prediction down by 0.194, the low income by 0.121 and the young age by 0.052, ending at 0.083. The three contributions add up exactly from 0.450 to 0.083, which is the efficiency property. Shapley valueExam favourite φj = ΣS ⊆ F ∖ j [ |S|! (|F| − |S| − 1)! / |F|! ] · [ v(S ∪ j) − v(S) ] v(S) is the average prediction when only the features in S are known. EfficiencyExam favourite f(x) = φ0 + Σj φj φ0 is the base value, the average prediction. Cost of the exact sum 2|F| coalitions 3 features need 8, 20 features already need 1,048,576. Going deeper: Explanations are not causes A Shapley value says how the model used a feature, not what would happen in the world if the feature changed. With correlated features the credit is split in ways that depend on the background data. Report explanations together with the background you used, and never read them as advice such as “lower your age”. For actionable advice, counterfactual explanations search for the smallest change to changeable features that flips the decision. Lesson 3 of 8"],[7,"fair","Is the model fair to every group?","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Hardt, Price and Srebro, Equality of opportunity in supervised learning (2016), US EEOC Uniform Guidelines on Employee Selection Procedures (1978), and Barocas, Hardt and Narayanan, Fairness and Machine Learning (2023). What it is A fairness audit computes the same rates separately for each group of people, such as sex, age band or ethnicity, and compares them. Demographic parity asks for equal approval rates. Equal opportunity asks for equal true positive rates: people who would repay are approved equally often in every group. Equalized odds also asks for equal false positive rates. Why we need it Leaving the group column out does not make a model fair. Other features such as postcode or income carry the same information, a proxy, and the model learns the old pattern from them. Lending, hiring and housing laws in many countries forbid unjustified differences between groups. How it works Keep the group labels aside for auditing, even when the model may not use them. Compute selection rate, TPR and FPR per group on test data, compare them with a chosen rule, and repeat the audit whenever the model or the data changes. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 fairness_audit.py. In the exam With one threshold of 0.5 group B is approved 33 percent of the time against 64 percent for group A, a disparate impact of 0.52, far below the four fifths rule of 0.8. Among people who did repay, B was approved 48 percent of the time and A 75 percent. Lowering B’s threshold to 0.38 closed the TPR gap, raised the ratio to 0.92 and cost only 0.010 accuracy (0.647 to 0.637). Disparate impact ratioExam favourite DI = P(ŷ = 1 | B) / P(ŷ = 1 | A) The four fifths rule flags DI below 0.8. Equal opportunity gapExam favourite TPRA − TPRB, with TPR = P(ŷ = 1 | y = 1) Zero means qualified people are treated alike. Equalized odds TPRA = TPRB and FPRA = FPRB Both kinds of error are shared equally. Lesson 4 of 8"],[7,"tools","SHAP and Fairlearn on your own computer","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: SHAP documentation (TreeExplainer, plots), and Fairlearn documentation (MetricFrame, ThresholdOptimizer). What it is SHAP is the standard Python library for Shapley explanations. Fairlearn computes metrics by group with MetricFrame and offers mitigation methods such as ThresholdOptimizer, which picks a threshold per group to meet a fairness rule. Why we need it Hand written loops teach the idea, but real projects need tested, fast tools whose numbers others can reproduce. Both libraries are open source and work with any scikit-learn model. How it works Install both packages, run the program, then add the plot lines to save a beeswarm chart (every row, every feature) and a waterfall chart (one row, from base value to prediction). Run this one on your own computer. It needs the shap and fairlearn packages, which the browser version of Python cannot install. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 shap_fairlearn.py. Remember TreeExplainer works in log odds for this model, and for the first applicant 0.027 + 0.489 + 0.027 + 0.401 = 0.943, exactly the model’s own log odds. Postcode gets a small mean |SHAP| of 0.055, yet the audit still shows a TPR gap of 0.196 between groups, because income also differs between groups. ThresholdOptimizer cut the gap to 0.018 while overall accuracy went from 0.672 to 0.665. Install SHAP and Fairlearn and run the program macOS Activate your project environment. Both packages publish ready built wheels for Apple Silicon and Intel Macs, so no compiler is needed. If pip starts compiling shap from source, your pip is old: upgrade it and try again. Run the program. This page was built with shap 0.54.1 and fairlearn 0.15.0. To keep the plots, add the lines shown below the panels. Open the PNG files with Preview. Terminal (macOS)shell source .venv/bin/activate python -m pip install --upgrade pip pip install shap fairlearn scikit-learn matplotlib python -c \"import shap, fairlearn; print(shap.__version__, fairlearn.__version__)\" python shap_fairlearn.py Windows 10 and 11 Activate the environment in PowerShell. Use python -m pip so the packages go into the active environment and not into another Python on the path. An error that mentions Microsoft Visual C++ 14.0 means pip found no wheel for your Python version and tried to compile. Use a Python version that shap lists on its PyPI page, or install the Microsoft C++ Build Tools. Plots saved with the lines below open in the Photos app. PowerShell (Windows)shell .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 python -m pip install --upgrade pip python -m pip install shap fairlearn scikit-learn matplotlib python -c \"import shap, fairlearn; print(shap.__version__, fairlearn.__version__)\" python shap_fairlearn.py Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) The same as macOS. On a server with no screen, matplotlib cannot open a window, so keep the matplotlib.use(\"Agg\") line from the snippet below or set MPLBACKEND=Agg before running. Terminal (Linux)shell source .venv/bin/activate pip install shap fairlearn scikit-learn matplotlib python -c \"import shap, fairlearn; print(shap.__version__, fairlearn.__version__)\" MPLBACKEND=Agg python shap_fairlearn.py ChromeOS (Chromebook) Install inside the Linux development environment. The saved PNG files appear in the Files app under Linux files, where you can open them with the ChromeOS image viewer. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell source .venv/bin/activate uv pip install shap fairlearn scikit-learn matplotlib python -c \"import shap, fairlearn; print(shap.__version__, fairlearn.__version__)\" python shap_fairlearn.py Add to the end of shap_fairlearn.py to save the plotspython 1import matplotlib2matplotlib.use(\"Agg\") # draw into files, no window needed3import matplotlib.pyplot as plt45sv.feature_names = names6shap.plots.beeswarm(sv, show=False) # every applicant, every feature7plt.savefig(\"shap_beeswarm.png\","],[7,"tools","SHAP and Fairlearn on your own computer","dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")8plt.close()9shap.plots.waterfall(sv[0], show=False) # one applicant, base value to prediction10plt.savefig(\"shap_waterfall.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\") Line 2 The Agg backend writes image files and needs no screen, so it also works on servers and in CI. Line 6 Each dot is one applicant, coloured by the feature value and placed by its SHAP value. Line 9 One applicant, bar by bar from the base value to the final log odds. Lesson 5 of 8"],[7,"tradeoff","Fixing unfairness, and why you cannot have every kind","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Chouldechova, Fair prediction with disparate impact (2017), and Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan, Inherent trade-offs in the fair determination of risk scores (2017). Three places to mitigate bias Before training (pre processing): fix the data. Collect more examples of the smaller group, reweight rows, or remove proxies that carry no real signal. During training (in processing): add a fairness constraint or penalty to the loss, for example Fairlearn’s ExponentiatedGradient. After training (post processing): choose a threshold per group, as ThresholdOptimizer did. It needs the group at decision time, which some laws do not allow. Whatever you choose, measure accuracy and every fairness metric again on fresh test data, and record the decision and its reasons. What it is An impossibility result says that when the groups have different base rates (shares of real positives), no imperfect classifier can be calibrated and also have equal false positive and false negative rates in every group. Why we need it Fairness is a choice between definitions that conflict, not a box you tick. Teams must pick the definition that fits the harm in their setting, and say so openly. How it works Ask which error hurts people most. Refusing a good borrower points to equal opportunity. Wrongly flagging an innocent person points to equal false positive rates. Then document the choice in the model card. Chouldechova’s identityExam favourite FPR = [p / (1 − p)] · [(1 − PPV) / PPV] · (1 − FNR) p is the base rate. With equal PPV and FNR, different p forces different FPR. Fairness rules compared Demographic parity Equal opportunity Equalized odds Calibration by group Asks for equal Approval rate TPR TPR and FPR Meaning of a score Uses true labels No Yes Yes Yes Fits when Labels themselves are biased Missing a qualified person is the main harm Both errors harm people Scores are read as probabilities Main cost Can approve unqualified people FPR may differ Usually lowers accuracy most Rates may differ between groups Bottom line: With different base rates, calibration by group conflicts with equalized odds. Choose one and explain why. Lesson 6 of 8"],[7,"cards","Model cards and responsible release","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Mitchell and colleagues, Model cards for model reporting (2019), Gebru and colleagues, Datasheets for datasets (2021), the EU AI Act (Regulation 2024/1689) and GDPR Article 22. What it is A model card is a short document shipped with every model version. It states what the model is for and not for, the training data, metrics overall and per group, fairness results, known limits and who to contact. A datasheet does the same for a dataset. Why we need it People who reuse a model rarely know how it was built. Regulators increasingly ask for this record: the EU AI Act lists credit scoring and hiring among high risk uses that need documentation and human oversight, and GDPR Article 22 gives people rights around decisions made only by automated means. How it works Generate the card from the tracking run and the audit, so the numbers cannot drift from the real model. Store it next to the model in the registry (MLOps 3.1) and make the promotion gate fail if the card or the fairness check is missing. Model card section Example for the loan model Intended use Rank loan applications for human review. Not for automatic refusal. Training data 20,000 past applications, 2019 to 2024, one country Metrics Accuracy 0.637 overall, reported per group Fairness Equal opportunity gap 0.00 after group thresholds, disparate impact 0.92 Explanations SHAP top three reasons sent with every refusal Limits Not tested on self employed applicants. Retrain if PSI of income passes 0.25 (MLOps 3.2). Remember Explainability and fairness checks belong in the same CI pipeline as accuracy tests (MLOps 2.3). A gate such as “fail if disparate impact is below 0.8 or the TPR gap is above 0.05” stops an unfair model from being promoted, in the same way as a failing unit test. Lesson 7 of 8"],[7,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths Ask which inputs a model relies on, and check whether it treats different groups of people alike. Compute permutation importance and Shapley values, audit selection rate, TPR and FPR per group, and apply the four fifths rule. Use SHAP and Fairlearn, choose and defend a fairness definition, mitigate bias before, during or after training, and write model cards. Explain models with correlated features and causal structure, prove trade-offs between fairness rules, and audit large language and vision models for bias. Beginner Ask what drives a model and who it affects. Intermediate Explain single predictions and audit groups. Expert Ship models with explanations, fairness gates and model cards. Real world scenario: A refused applicant asks why A bank’s model refuses a loan. The applicant asks for the reason. The team computes SHAP values for that one decision: debt ratio −0.194, income −0.121, age −0.052. They send the two main reasons, debt ratio and income, in plain words. Because age is a protected attribute in many lending laws, the quarterly audit also checks that approval rates by age band pass the four fifths rule. Lesson 8 of 8"],[7,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Permutation importance on test data measures real dependence (worst radius 0.170). Impurity importance is computed on training data and still credited a random ID with 0.018. Shapley values split one prediction exactly: base 0.450, then −0.121, −0.194 and −0.052, ending at 0.083. TreeExplainer computes them exactly for trees. Dropping the group column does not remove bias, because proxies carry it. One threshold gave a disparate impact of 0.52 and a TPR gap of 0.28. A per group threshold closed the TPR gap for 0.010 accuracy. With different base rates, calibration and equal error rates cannot all hold. Install shap and fairlearn per OS, save plots with the Agg backend, and ship a model card with every version. Next: Coming next: designing a whole ML system end to end, and revising MLOps for interviews and exams. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: permutation importance from scores, exact Shapley values for two features, and a disparate impact ratio by group.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[8,"","Designing an ML system end to end","Sizing with QPS and Little's law, serving modes, skew, shadow and canary releases, A/B test sample size and peeking, and a local load test. · ML system design · capacity planning · Little's law · feature store · training-serving skew · canary release · A/B testing · load testing Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 4: Design and certify›ML system design Step 4 · Topic 1 of 2 · Expert Turn requirements into traffic, latency and storage numbers, choose batch, online or streaming serving, prevent training-serving skew, release with shadow and canary steps, run an honest A/B test, and load test a model service on your own computer. About 60 min8 lessons2 animations6 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Start with the problem, then size the system 2Batch, online and streaming predictions 3Shadow, canary and rollback 4A/B testing a new model 5Load test your model service on your own computer 6Worked design: real time fraud detection 7From beginner to expert 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[8,"frame","Start with the problem, then size the system","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Huyen, Designing Machine Learning Systems (2022) chapters 1 and 2, and Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications (2017) chapter 1. A framework for any ML system design question Clarify the goal: the business metric (for example watch time or fraud losses) and the ML task that serves it (ranking, classification). Write the requirements: how many users, how fast an answer must be, how fresh the data, what a wrong answer costs, privacy and fairness limits. Design the data: where labels come from, which features, how they are computed for training and for serving. Choose the model: start with a simple baseline, then the model that fits the latency and the data. Design serving: batch or online, one stage or retrieval then ranking, and how many replicas. Plan evaluation: offline metrics first, then a shadow run, a canary and an online A/B test. Plan operations: monitoring, retraining and rollback (MLOps 3.1 and 3.2). What it is ML system design is the plan for everything around a model: data pipelines, features, training, serving, evaluation and monitoring, sized for real traffic. Why we need it In production the model is a small part of the code. Most failures come from the system around it: slow serving, features that differ between training and serving, or a rollout with no way back. Interviews at large companies test exactly this. How it works Turn every requirement into a number, do quick arithmetic for traffic, latency and storage, and let those numbers choose the design. Python # Back of the envelope sizing for a real time recommendation model, the first step of an ML system design.import mathdaily_users = 10_000_000# people who open the app each dayrequests_per_user = 20# feed refreshes per user per daypeak_factor = 3# the busiest hour is 3 times the daily averagelatency_s = 0.040# one prediction takes 40 ms on one workerworkers_per_replica = 4# parallel workers in one containerheadroom = 0.6# run replicas at 60 percent load so a spike or a lost replica is safeavg_qps = daily_users * requests_per_user / 86_400peak_qps = avg_qps * peak_factorper_replica = workers_per_replica / latency_s # Little's law: throughput = concurrency / timereplicas = math.ceil(peak_qps / (per_replica * headroom))print(f'average {avg_qps:,.0f} requests/s, peak {peak_qps:,.0f} requests/s')print(f'one replica serves {per_replica:.0f} requests/s, so {replicas} replicas at {headroom:.0%} load')candidates, ranked = 1_000_000, 50# catalogue size and items shownrank_cost = 1e-6# the ranking model needs 1 microsecond per itemprint(f'Ranking all {candidates:,} items would take {candidates * rank_cost * 1000:,.0f} ms per request.', f'Retrieve 500 candidates cheaply first, and ranking takes {500 * rank_cost * 1000:.1f} ms. Show the top {ranked}.')bytes_per_log = 2_000# features, prediction and id of one requestper_day_gb = daily_users * requests_per_user * bytes_per_log / 1e9print(f'Prediction logs: {per_day_gb:,.0f} GB a day, {per_day_gb * 90 / 1000:,.1f} TB for 90 days of training data') Output average 2,315 requests/s, peak 6,944 requests/s one replica serves 100 requests/s, so 116 replicas at 60% load Ranking all 1,000,000 items would take 1,000 ms per request. Retrieve 500 candidates cheaply first, and ranking takes 0.5 ms. Show the top 50. Prediction logs: 400 GB a day, 36.0 TB for 90 days of training data Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 capacity_plan.py. In the exam Ten million daily users with 20 requests each average 2,315 requests per second and peak at 6,944. One replica with 4 workers at 40 ms handles 100 per second, so 116 replicas keep each at 60 percent load. Ranking a million items per request would take a full second, which is why recommenders retrieve 500 candidates first. Average loadExam favourite QPS = daily requests / 86,400 A day has 86,400 seconds. Little’s lawExam favourite L = λ × W Requests in the system ="],[8,"frame","Start with the problem, then size the system","arrival rate × time each one spends. So throughput = workers / latency. Replicas replicas = ⌈ peak QPS / (per replica QPS × target load) ⌉ A target load of 0.6 leaves room for spikes and a failed replica. Lesson 2 of 8"],[8,"serve","Batch, online and streaming predictions","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Huyen (2022) chapter 7, Covington, Adams and Sargin, Deep neural networks for YouTube recommendations (2016), and Breck and colleagues, The ML test score (2017). Three ways to deliver predictions Batch Online (on request) Streaming When it predicts On a schedule, for everyone When a request arrives When an event arrives Latency Hours, results read from a table Milliseconds Seconds Feature freshness As old as the last run Request data plus stored features Features updated from the event stream Example Nightly churn scores for the sales team Fraud check during a payment Live delivery time estimates Main risk Stale scores, wasted work for inactive users Tail latency and cost Complex pipelines (Kafka, Flink) Bottom line: Many systems mix them: batch for heavy features, online for the final score. What it is Training-serving skew means the model sees features in production that were computed differently from the ones it was trained on. A feature store keeps one definition of each feature and serves it both to training jobs (as of a past date) and to the live service (the latest value). Why we need it Skew is silent. The service answers, the offline score looked great, and the live accuracy is poor. It is one of the most common reasons why a model that won offline loses online. How it works Compute every feature with one shared function or a feature store, log the features the service actually used, and add a parity test that sends the same raw rows through the training path and the serving path. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 skew_check.py. Common mistake The model scored 0.730 offline. The serving code filled missing spend with 0 instead of the training median and received spend in pence, not pounds, so live accuracy fell to 0.550 and the mean predicted churn collapsed from 0.435 to 0.124. A parity test on the same raw rows finds the difference on 100 percent of rows before release. Going deeper: Two stage recommenders Large recommenders answer in two stages. Retrieval picks a few hundred candidates out of millions with a cheap method, such as nearest neighbours of a user embedding in a vector index. Ranking scores only those candidates with a heavier model that uses many features. Some systems add a third re-ranking step for business rules such as diversity and freshness. The capacity program above shows why: at 1 microsecond an item, ranking everything takes 1,000 ms, ranking 500 candidates takes 0.5 ms. Lesson 3 of 8"],[8,"rollout","Shadow, canary and rollback","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Google, The Site Reliability Workbook (2018), chapter 16, Canarying releases. What it is A shadow deployment sends a copy of live requests to the new model and logs its answers without showing them. A canary gives the new model to a small share of users and grows the share step by step. A guardrail is a metric with a limit, such as error rate or latency, checked after every step. Why we need it Offline tests never cover everything real users do. A gradual rollout limits how many people a bad model can hurt, and an automatic rollback fixes it in seconds instead of hours. How it works Run in shadow first to compare answers and latency. Then route 1, 5, 25, 50 and 100 percent of traffic, check the guardrails at each step, and keep the old version deployed until the new one has been stable for a while. Strategy Users see the new model Good for Watch out for Shadow No Latency, crashes, agreement with the old model Cannot measure user reactions, doubles compute Canary A small growing share Catching bugs with little harm Small shares need time to show rare problems Blue-green All at once, with an instant switch back Simple, fast rollback No gradual exposure A/B test A fixed random half (or share) Measuring the effect on a business metric Needs enough users and a fixed plan Multi-armed bandit More traffic to the version that is winning Many variants, short campaigns Harder to read the final effect Lesson 4 of 8"],[8,"abtest","A/B testing a new model","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Kohavi, Tang and Xu, Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments (2020) chapters 2, 17 and 18. What it is An A/B test assigns users at random to the old model (A) or the new one (B) and compares a metric such as conversion. A two proportion z test says whether the difference is larger than chance. The sample size is fixed before the test from the smallest lift worth detecting. Why we need it Offline gains often vanish online, because users react to the model and the offline metric is only a proxy. A randomised test is the only reliable way to measure the real effect, but only if it is run honestly. How it works Choose the metric, the smallest lift that matters (minimum detectable effect, MDE), α and power. Compute n per group, run until both groups reach n, then test once. Watch guardrail metrics during the test, but do not stop early because the p value looks good. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 ab_test.py. In the exam To detect a lift from 5.0 to 5.5 percent with α = 0.05 and 80 percent power you need 31,235 users per group. In 2,000 A/A tests a single look at the end gave 4.4 percent false alarms, close to the promised 5. Stopping on the first day with p < 0.05 gave 22.0 percent, more than four times as many. Sample size per groupExam favourite n = (z1−α/2 + z1−β)² · 2 p̄(1 − p̄) / δ² p̄ is the average rate, δ the lift. Halving δ needs four times the users. Two proportion z testExam favourite z = (pB − pA) / √( p(1 − p)(1/nA + 1/nB) ) p is the pooled rate of both groups. Many looks P(at least one false alarm) ≤ 1 − (1 − α)k An upper limit for k looks. Real looks share data, so the true rate is lower but still far above α. Lesson 5 of 8"],[8,"load","Load test your model service on your own computer","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Python documentation: http.server, urllib.request and concurrent.futures. What it is A load test sends many requests at once to a service and records throughput (requests per second) and latency percentiles at growing numbers of simultaneous users. Why we need it Capacity plans are guesses until you measure. A load test shows the real requests per second of one replica, the number you divide the peak by, and the point where latency starts to climb. How it works Start the service from MLOps 2.2 or the Docker image from MLOps 2.3, run the load test against it, and find the number of users where requests per second stop growing. Use that, with headroom, in the capacity plan. Run this one on your own computer. A load test needs real network calls to a server on your machine, which a browser page cannot make. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 load_test.py. In the exam The built-in demo server can work on 4 requests at once, each taking 20 ms, so Little’s law predicts 4 / 0.020 = 200 requests per second. The test gets close to 200 with 4 users and stays just under 200 with 8 and 16 users, while the median latency rises from about 21 ms to about 40 ms and then higher. Past the saturation point, more users only add waiting time. Timings change a little on every run. Start the model service and load test it macOS Start Docker Desktop, then start the image you built in MLOps 2.3. Without Docker, start the FastAPI service from MLOps 2.2 with uvicorn fastapi_serve:app --port 8000 --workers 4. Both accept the same flower fields. Wait until the health check answers, then run the load test in a second Terminal window. Compare the table with the real run below. Apple Silicon Macs are usually faster than the cloud machine used for this page. Terminal (macOS)shell docker run -d --rm --name lt -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health python3 load_test.py --url http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict docker stop lt Windows 10 and 11 Start Docker Desktop (it uses WSL 2), then run the container in PowerShell. Or start the FastAPI service with uvicorn as in MLOps 2.2, which also runs on Windows. Use 127.0.0.1 rather than localhost. On Windows localhost may try the IPv6 address first, which adds a delay to every request and spoils the timings. In PowerShell curl is an alias of Invoke-WebRequest, so type curl.exe to get the real curl. PowerShell (Windows)shell docker run -d --rm --name lt -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 curl.exe http://127.0.0.1:8000/health python load_test.py --url http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict docker stop lt Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) Docker commands need sudo unless your user is in the docker group (log out and in again after adding it). Close other heavy programs while measuring, because they share the same CPU. Terminal (Linux)shell sudo usermod -aG docker $USER # once, then log out and in docker run -d --rm --name lt -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health python3 load_test.py --url http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict docker stop lt ChromeOS (Chromebook) Docker Desktop does not run on ChromeOS. Start the FastAPI service from MLOps 2.2 with uvicorn inside the Linux environment and test it from a second Linux terminal. The Linux environment has fewer CPU cores than the Chromebook itself, so expect lower numbers. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell source .venv/bin/activate uvicorn fastapi_serve:app --port 8000 --workers 2 & python3 load_test.py --url http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict kill %1 # stop the server A real run against the Docker image from MLOps 2.3 (Linux)shell $ docker run -d --rm --name lt -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 $ curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health {\"status\":\"ok\",\"model_version\":\"1.0.0\"} $ python load_test.py --url http://127.0.0.1:8000/predict --requests 200 users req/s p50 ms p95 ms p99 ms 1 446 2.1 2.7 3.6 2 792 2.3 3.4 4.2 4 1060"],[8,"load","Load test your model service on your own computer","3.5 5.8 7.9 8 1182 6.3 10.3 11.7 16 1180 12.1 19.1 20.5 $ docker stop lt Remember The real FastAPI container from MLOps 2.3 levelled off at about 1,180 requests per second from 8 users, while its p50 went from 2.1 ms to 12.1 ms at 16 users. For the 6,944 requests per second peak, that machine would need about 10 such replicas at 60 percent load, a number you can now defend. Lesson 6 of 8"],[8,"case","Worked design: real time fraud detection","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Real world scenario: A payment company asks for a fraud model Every card payment must be approved or blocked within 100 ms, at a peak of 3,000 payments a second. Fraud is about 0.1 percent of payments. A missed fraud costs the full amount, and a wrongly blocked payment costs an angry customer. Fraud labels arrive weeks later, when customers dispute charges. Part of the design Choice Why Chapter Metric Fraud losses saved, with a cap on wrongly blocked good payments Accuracy is useless at 0.1 percent fraud ML 2.x metrics Features Payment amount, merchant, card history counts over 1 hour and 30 days History counts come from a feature store, updated from the payment stream This chapter Model Gradient boosted trees, a few milliseconds per payment Strong on tabular data, fast, explainable with SHAP MLOps 3.3 Serving Online, inside the payment flow, rules as a fallback Must decide before the payment completes MLOps 2.2 Rollout Shadow for two weeks, then canary with guardrails on block rate Fraud labels are slow, so start with safe signals This chapter Monitoring Score PSI, block rate, latency p99, dispute rate when labels arrive Fraudsters adapt, so concept drift is certain MLOps 3.2 Retraining Weekly through the tested pipeline and the registry gate New fraud patterns appear all the time MLOps 3.1 Remember Interviewers look for the trade-offs, not one right answer. Say what you would measure, what you would try first, and how you would know it failed. Lesson 7 of 8"],[8,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths See a model as one part of a bigger system that collects data, answers users and needs watching. Size a system with QPS, Little’s law and replicas, choose batch or online serving, and load test a service. Prevent training-serving skew, roll out with shadow and canary steps, and run an A/B test with a fixed sample size. Design multi stage recommenders and real time feature pipelines, use sequential tests and variance reduction such as CUPED, and lead design reviews. Beginner Explain the parts of an ML system. Intermediate Size, serve and safely release a model. Expert Design and defend a complete ML system in an interview or at work. Lesson 8 of 8"],[8,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Turn requirements into numbers: 10 million users × 20 requests is 2,315 per second on average, 6,944 at peak, 116 replicas at 60 percent load. Choose batch, online or streaming serving. Retrieve cheaply, then rank: 500 candidates take 0.5 ms instead of 1,000 ms. Training-serving skew took accuracy from 0.730 to 0.550. Share one feature function or a feature store and add a parity test. Release in steps: shadow, canary with guardrails, automatic rollback. A/B test with a fixed n (31,235 per group). Peeking raised false alarms from 4.4 to 22 percent. Load test on your own computer: throughput levels off at workers / latency, and after that only waiting time grows. Next: Coming next: revising every MLOps chapter for interviews and certification exams. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: replicas from traffic, A/B test sample size, and a canary guardrail check.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[9,"","MLOps revision and certification practice","The whole subject as one loop, maturity levels, a release gate across all chapters, a capstone, cloud service mapping and exam practice. · MLOps revision · maturity levels · release gate · capstone · certification · Vertex AI · SageMaker · Azure ML · interview Brainy BytesMLOps learning path MLOps›Step 4: Design and certify›Revision and practice Step 4 · Topic 2 of 2 · Expert Revise the whole subject as one loop, place teams on the MLOps maturity levels, combine every chapter in one release gate, run a capstone project on your own computer, map each tool to Google Cloud, AWS and Azure, and practise interview and exam questions. About 50 min8 lessons2 animations6 formulas10 worked problems14 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The whole subject in one loop 2MLOps maturity levels 3One release gate that uses every chapter 4Capstone: the whole lifecycle on your computer 5The same ideas on cloud platforms 6Interview questions with model answers 7From beginner to expert 8Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 8"],[9,"loop","The whole subject in one loop","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. The whole MLOps subject in one loopEvery chapter is one stage of a model’s life. Walk the loop in order to revise, or follow a real incident and see which chapters you use and in what order. Step 0 of 10 Walk Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this A drift incident: monitoring raises the alarm, explanations find the cause, the registry limits harm, and the pipeline, CI and canary ship the fix. Chapter by chapter: one key idea and one number to remember for each. What it is Revision here means seeing each chapter as one stage of a model’s life, and knowing the one idea, the one number and the one trap that go with it. Why we need it Exams and interviews rarely ask about one chapter alone. They describe a situation, such as a model that got worse, and expect you to pick the right tool from several chapters in the right order. How it works Walk the loop chapter by chapter, then follow the incident path. For each stage, say the idea out loud, recall the number, and name the trap in the table below. Chapter Key idea Classic trap 1.1 Setup A venv per project, exact versions in requirements.txt Installing into the system Python, committing .venv 1.2 Lifecycle Seeds, run records, data fingerprints Reporting the best seed instead of the spread 2.1 Pipelines Fit on training rows only, save one pipeline Scaling or selecting features before the split 2.2 Serving Validate input, 422 for bad requests Loading the model inside the request handler 2.3 Docker, CI/CD Requirements before code, gate every release Running as root, testing only the code and not the model 3.1 Tracking Log every run, aliases for promotion Choosing the best of many runs on the validation set and reporting that score 3.2 Monitoring PSI for size, labels for accuracy Trusting KS p values on huge samples, missing concept drift 3.3 Explain, fair Permutation importance on test data, rates by group Thinking that dropping the group column removes bias 4.1 System design Numbers first, safe rollout, honest A/B test Stopping a test the first day p < 0.05 Lesson 2 of 8"],[9,"maturity","MLOps maturity levels","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Google Cloud, MLOps: continuous delivery and automation pipelines in machine learning. What it is A maturity level describes how much of the ML lifecycle runs automatically. At level 0 people move data and models by hand. At level 1 an automated pipeline retrains and delivers models (continuous training). At level 2 CI/CD also tests, builds and deploys the pipeline code itself. Why we need it It gives teams a shared way to say where they are and what to build next. Exams use it to test whether you know which tool solves which bottleneck. How it works Move from level 0 to 1 when models go stale between manual retrains. Move from 1 to 2 when many people change the pipeline code and releases of the pipeline itself become risky. Level What is automated What starts a new model Tools from this subject 0 Manual Only serving A person, often months apart Notebooks, a saved model file (2.1) 1 Pipeline automation Data checks, training, validation, delivery Schedule, new data or a drift alert Pipelines (2.1), tracking and registry (3.1), monitoring (3.2) 2 CI/CD automation Also testing, building and deploying the pipeline Also every code change Docker and GitHub Actions (2.3), registry gates (3.1) Remember Level 2 is not always the goal. A small team with one model retrained monthly may be well served by level 1. Match the automation to how often the data and the code change. Lesson 3 of 8"],[9,"gate","One release gate that uses every chapter","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. What it is A release gate is the list of checks a new model must pass before it can replace the champion: feature parity, a quality gain on the same holdout, fairness limits, drift of its scores and practical limits such as file size. Why we need it Each chapter adds a check. Putting them in one gate means no model reaches users without passing all of them, and the gate’s output is the evidence for the model card. How it works Run the gate in CI (MLOps 2.3) on every candidate, block promotion on any FAIL, and store the report with the registered version (MLOps 3.1). Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 release_gate.py. In the exam Challenger 1 has the higher AUC, 0.0498 above the champion, because it uses postcode and the past labels favoured group A. The gate blocks it: the equal opportunity gap is 0.227 and the disparate impact is 0.48. Challenger 2 drops postcode, still gains 0.0125 AUC, passes all six checks and is promoted. A higher score on biased labels is not a better model. PSIExam favourite PSI = Σ (q − p) ln(q / p) 0.1 watch, 0.25 drift (3.2). Little’s lawExam favourite workers = request rate × latency Sizing workers and replicas (2.2, 4.1). Disparate impactExam favourite DI = rateB / rateA ≥ 0.8 Four fifths rule (3.3). Error budget and burn rateExam favourite budget = (1 − SLO) × requests, burn = actual / allowed A burn rate of 14.4 spends 2 percent of a 30 day budget in an hour (3.2). Shapley efficiency f(x) = φ0 + Σ φj Contributions add up to the prediction (3.3). A/B sample size n = (za + zb)² 2p̄(1 − p̄) / δ² Fix n before the test (4.1). Lesson 4 of 8"],[9,"capstone","Capstone: the whole lifecycle on your computer","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. What it is The capstone program runs the subject end to end in one file: data with a fingerprint, a leak free pipeline, a tracked run, a registered model with a champion alias, a validated API tested with FastAPI’s test client, a drift check and a model card. Why we need it Reading about each step is not the same as wiring them together. Running the capstone on your own computer, then changing it, is the best revision for a practical exam or a take home task. How it works Run it, read every file it writes, then try the changes listed under the panels: each one exercises a different chapter. Run this one on your own computer. It writes a project folder with a model file, a run log and a model card that you will want to open and keep, and it needs FastAPI. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 mlops_capstone.py. Remember The test AUC is 0.7038 on synthetic data. The API returns 200 for a good request, 422 for a negative tenure and still 200 for the unseen plan family, because the encoder ignores unknown categories. After a 20 percent price rise the PSI of monthly_bill is 0.334, so the drift check says drift. The model card records all of it. Set up a fresh environment and run the capstone macOS Make a fresh folder and environment for the capstone, so nothing from earlier chapters hides a missing package. Run the program. It creates the folder capstone next to itself. Open MODEL_CARD.md in any editor, or preview it in VS Code with Shift Command V. Terminal (macOS)shell mkdir ~/mlops-capstone && cd ~/mlops-capstone python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate pip install scikit-learn pandas joblib fastapi httpx python mlops_capstone.py cat capstone/MODEL_CARD.md cat capstone/runs.jsonl Windows 10 and 11 Create the folder and environment in PowerShell. If activation is blocked, run Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned once (MLOps 1.1). Run the program, then read the files with Get-Content, or open the folder in VS Code and press Ctrl Shift V on the model card. PowerShell (Windows)shell mkdir $HOME\\mlops-capstone; cd $HOME\\mlops-capstone py -m venv .venv; .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1 python -m pip install scikit-learn pandas joblib fastapi httpx python mlops_capstone.py Get-Content capstone\\MODEL_CARD.md Get-Content capstone\\runs.jsonl Linux (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora) The same as macOS. On Debian and Ubuntu install the venv module first if python3 -m venv complains that ensurepip is not available. Terminal (Linux)shell sudo apt install python3-venv # Debian and Ubuntu, once mkdir ~/mlops-capstone && cd ~/mlops-capstone python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate pip install scikit-learn pandas joblib fastapi httpx python mlops_capstone.py cat capstone/MODEL_CARD.md ChromeOS (Chromebook) Run it in the Linux development environment. The capstone folder appears in the Files app under Linux files, where the Text app can open MODEL_CARD.md. Terminal (ChromeOS Linux)shell mkdir ~/mlops-capstone && cd ~/mlops-capstone python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate uv pip install scikit-learn pandas joblib fastapi httpx python mlops_capstone.py cat capstone/MODEL_CARD.md Changes to try, one chapter each Change SEED and run again: the data fingerprint and AUC change, and runs.jsonl now has two records to compare (1.2, 3.1). Train a second model with C = 0.1, save it as churn-v2.joblib and move the champion alias only if its AUC is higher (3.1). Add a test file that posts a request with a missing plan and checks for status 422 (2.2, 2.3). Write a Dockerfile for the API part, following MLOps 2.3, and run it with docker run -p 8000:8000. Change the price rise to 1.05 and see the drift verdict return to stable (3.2). Lesson 5 of 8"],[9,"cloud","The same ideas on cloud platforms","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Source: Public product documentation of Google Cloud Vertex AI, Amazon SageMaker and Azure Machine Learning. What it is Cloud ML platforms offer managed versions of every tool in this subject. The names differ, but the ideas are the same ones you have practised with open source tools. Why we need it Cloud certifications, such as Google Cloud’s Professional Machine Learning Engineer, AWS’s Machine Learning Engineer Associate and Microsoft’s Azure Data Scientist Associate, ask you to pick the right managed service for a situation. Knowing the idea first makes the service names easy to learn. How it works Learn the idea with the open source tool, then map it to the service of the platform you use. Exam guides and service names change, so always read the current official exam guide before you book. Idea (chapter) Open source here Google Cloud AWS Azure Pipelines (2.1, 2.3) scikit-learn Pipeline, GitHub Actions Vertex AI Pipelines SageMaker Pipelines Azure ML pipelines Serving (2.2) FastAPI, Docker Vertex AI endpoints SageMaker endpoints Azure ML online endpoints Tracking (3.1) MLflow Vertex AI Experiments SageMaker with managed MLflow Azure ML with MLflow Registry (3.1) MLflow Model Registry Vertex AI Model Registry SageMaker Model Registry Azure ML model registry Monitoring (3.2) PSI job, cron Vertex AI Model Monitoring SageMaker Model Monitor Azure ML model monitoring Explain and fairness (3.3) SHAP, Fairlearn Vertex Explainable AI SageMaker Clarify Responsible AI dashboard Feature store (4.1) one shared feature function Vertex AI Feature Store SageMaker Feature Store Azure ML managed feature store In the exam A common exam pattern: a scenario with stale models and manual retraining, and four services to choose from. The answer is the pipeline plus monitoring service of that platform, because the problem is level 0 maturity, not the model. Lesson 6 of 8"],[9,"interview","Interview questions with model answers","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Five classic MLOps interview questions Your model was great offline and poor online. Why? Check training-serving skew first (feature parity test), then leakage in the offline evaluation (fitting on all rows), then a shift between the offline data and live traffic, and finally whether the offline metric matches the business goal. How do you know when to retrain? Monitor input PSI and the score distribution daily, accuracy when labels arrive, and the business metric. Retrain on a schedule for slow drift or on a trigger when performance drops past a limit, always through the tested pipeline and the registry gate. How do you release a new model safely? Shadow first, then a canary with guardrails on errors and latency, then an A/B test with a sample size fixed in advance, keeping the old version ready for a one step rollback. How do you make results reproducible? Pin package versions, fix seeds, fingerprint the data, track every run with code version and parameters, and build the serving image from the same requirements. A regulator asks why someone was refused. What do you show? The model card, the fairness audit by group, and the SHAP explanation of that decision with the background data used, plus the version and run that produced the model. Real world scenario: A take home task in a hiring process A company sends a CSV file and asks for a churn model served as an API, with tests and a short report, in one weekend. A strong answer is the capstone shape: a fingerprint of the data, one pipeline, a tracked run, a FastAPI service with an input contract and tests, a Dockerfile, a drift check, and a one page model card. Reviewers mostly read the tests and the report, so keep them clear. Lesson 7 of 8"],[9,"levels","From beginner to expert","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. One idea, four depths Name the stages of a model’s life and the tool for each stage. Run the capstone, read its outputs, and explain each check of the release gate. Diagnose a failing model across chapters, place a team on the maturity levels, and map each tool to a cloud service. Design and defend a level 2 platform for many models and teams, with gates, monitoring and audits that regulators accept. Beginner Recall one idea and one number per chapter. Intermediate Combine the chapters in a release gate and a capstone. Expert Pass MLOps interviews and certification exams with confidence. Lesson 8 of 8"],[9,"recap","Chapter summary","Speed Reads the explanations aloud. Code and tables are skipped. Nine chapters form one loop: setup, lifecycle, pipelines, serving, Docker and CI/CD, tracking, monitoring, explainability and fairness, system design. Maturity: level 0 is manual, level 1 retrains through an automated pipeline, level 2 also tests and deploys the pipeline with CI/CD. One release gate combines parity, quality, fairness, drift and size checks. It blocked a higher AUC model that learned biased labels through postcode. The capstone wrote a run record, a registered model with an alias, a tested API, a drift report and a model card from one file. Cloud platforms rename the same ideas: Vertex AI, SageMaker and Azure ML each have pipelines, endpoints, tracking, a registry, monitoring and explainability. Next: You have finished the MLOps subject. Go back to any chapter through the loop above, or try the cheatsheet for a quick review before an exam. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a release gate, the current champion after promotions and rollbacks, and how long an error budget lasts.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[10,"","Cheatsheet","Every command, key idea, table, memory aid and common mistake from the MLOps chapters on one page, from setting up Python, Git and Docker on macOS, Windows, Linux and ChromeOS to reproducible projects, leak free pipelines, serving with Flask, FastAPI and Django, Docker and CI/CD, MLflow tracking and the model registry, drift monitoring, SHAP and fairness audits, and ML system design with canary releases and A/B tests. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · formula sheet · revision · PDF · download · quick reference · virtual environment · pip · random seed · Pipeline · data leakage · FastAPI · Dockerfile · layer cache · GitHub Actions · MLflow · model registry · PSI · error budget · SHAP · disparate impact · Little's law · canary · A/B sample size"],[10,"step-1","Step 1Set up and plan","1.1"],[10,"mlops-setup","Set up your computer for machine learning","Open chapter Python, virtual environments, Git, VS Code, Docker and GPUs set up on macOS, Windows, Linux and ChromeOS. Key ideas The toolbox Python 3.12 or newer, terminal, .venv per project, pip or uv, Git, VS Code, later Docker and a GPU or Colab. PATH The first match in PATH runs. activate puts .venv first. python -m pip keeps pip and python together. Per system macOS and Linux: python3, .venv/bin. Windows: python or py, .venv\\Scripts, execution policy. ChromeOS: Linux container, Debian steps. Reproducible pip freeze > requirements.txt, pip install -r requirements.txt. Commit requirements, ignore .venv. Never sudo pip. Docker Desktop on macOS and Windows (WSL 2), Engine on Linux, docker group, docker run hello-world. GPU NVIDIA: driver, nvidia-smi, CUDA build of PyTorch. Apple silicon: MPS. Chromebook: Colab or Kaggle. Tables Table: tool · what it is · why ml needs it Tool What it is Why ML needs it Python 3.12 or newer The language and its interpreter NumPy, pandas, scikit-learn and PyTorch need a recent Python Terminal A window where you type commands Installing, running scripts, Git, Docker Virtual environment A folder with its own Python and packages Each project keeps its own versions pip or uv Package installers Install libraries from PyPI, the Python package index Git Version control History of code and configs, teamwork, CI VS Code Editor with Python and Jupyter support Write, run and debug code and notebooks Docker Runs apps in containers Ship the model with everything it needs GPU or cloud notebook Fast parallel hardware Training deep networks in hours, not days Lesson: What you need and why Table: command · pip and venv · uv Command pip and venv uv Create an environment python3 -m venv .venv uv venv Install packages pip install pandas uv pip install pandas Install from a file pip install -r requirements.txt uv pip install -r requirements.txt Save exact versions pip freeze > requirements.txt uv pip freeze > requirements.txt Install a Python version Use the python.org installer uv python install 3.14 Lesson: Virtual environments and packages Table: problem · system · fix Problem System Fix python: command not found macOS, Linux, ChromeOS Use python3, or activate the .venv Typing python opens the Microsoft Store Windows Turn off the App execution aliases for python Running scripts is disabled on this system Windows PowerShell Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned error: externally-managed-environment Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora, ChromeOS Install inside a virtual environment, never with sudo pip ensurepip is not available Debian, Ubuntu, ChromeOS sudo apt install python3-venv A package installed but import fails All pip and python come from different places: use python -m pip Linux option missing in Settings ChromeOS Managed device: ask the administrator or use Colab Docker permission denied Linux, ChromeOS Join the docker group, then log out and back in Lesson: GPUs, the cloud and common problems Table: · venv and pip · uv · conda venv and pip uv conda Docker What it isolates Python packages Python packages and Python versions Python and non Python libraries The whole system: OS libraries, Python, packages Comes with Python Yes No, one small install No, a separate distribution No, a separate app Speed Fine Very fast Slower Slow to build, fast to start Lock file requirements.txt uv.lock or requirements.txt environment.yml Dockerfile plus requirements Best for Learning, small projects Everyday work, CI Science stacks with C and CUDA libraries Serving and deploying Lesson: venv, uv, conda and Docker compared Tips and traps Remember Run this check after each part of the setup. The output above is from the computer that built this page: Python 3.14.6 on Linux inside a virtual environment, with every package installed. Inside this web page, the same program runs in the browser’s own Python, so it reports a different system and that the web packages are not installed.Lesson: What you need and why In the exam Two"],[10,"mlops-setup","Set up your computer for machine learning","names, one idea: macOS, Linux and ChromeOS call it python3, Windows calls it python or py. Inside an activated virtual environment, python works everywhere.Lesson: Install Python and open a terminal In the exam The program shows what python -m venv creates: a bin folder (Scripts on Windows), include and lib folders, and pyvenv.cfg. The new python reports that it is inside a virtual environment, its packages go to the environment’s own site-packages, and include-system-site-packages = false means it cannot see packages installed for the whole computer.Lesson: Virtual environments and packages Remember The check finds two problems: NumPy 2.4.6 is installed but the file asks for 2.3.5, and MLflow is missing. Running pip install -r requirements.txt fixes both, which is exactly what a CI job or a new teammate does first.Lesson: Virtual environments and packages Remember If hello-world prints Hello from Docker!, the install works. Permission denied on /var/run/docker.sock on Linux means you have not logged out and back in since joining the docker group.Lesson: Docker on each system 1.2"],[10,"mlops-lifecycle","The MLOps lifecycle and reproducible projects","Open chapter The MLOps loop and maturity levels, seeds and run records, data and model versioning, and project layout. Key ideas MLOps Version, test, deploy, monitor and retrain ML. DevOps plus data and models. Levels 0 manual, 1 automated training pipeline (continuous training), 2 CI/CD for the pipeline. Reproducible Fix seeds, report spread over seeds, save run records: data fingerprint, params, metrics, versions. Versions Code in Git, data by hash (DVC), models in a registry, environment in requirements or images. Lineage. Tests Data, features, model quality and behaviour, infrastructure. Notebooks to tested functions. Why it matters Models decay as data changes. Hidden technical debt lives outside the model code. Formulas An ML systemExam favourite behaviour = code + data + model + configuration All four need versions. Lesson: What MLOps is Continuous trainingExam favourite new data → retrain → evaluate → register → deploy The loop of level 1. Lesson: What MLOps is Automation share automated stages / all stages A rough maturity measure. Lesson: What MLOps is Tables Table: level · what is automated · typical sign Level What is automated Typical sign 0: manual Nothing. Notebooks, files copied by hand A model is retrained a few times a year 1: ML pipeline automation Data, training, evaluation and registration run as one pipeline, retraining is triggered Continuous training (CT) on new data 2: CI/CD pipeline automation The pipeline itself is tested, built and deployed by CI/CD Many models updated safely every week Lesson: What MLOps is Table: what · versioned with · example What Versioned with Example Code and configs Git Commit a1b2c3d Data Fingerprint, DVC or lakeFS customers.csv, hash 25e2eb7b7a87 Environment requirements.txt, lock file, Docker image tag ml-api:1.4.0 Model Model registry churn-model version 7, stage production Experiments Tracking server MLflow run 3f9e, accuracy 0.981 Lesson: Versioning data and models Table: test type · checks · example Test type Checks Example Data tests Schema, ranges, missing values, label balance age between 0 and 120 Feature tests Transformations do what they claim Scaled column has mean near 0 on training data Model tests Quality floor, behaviour on known cases Accuracy at least 0.9, prediction unchanged when the name changes Infrastructure tests Pipeline runs end to end, model loads and serves Training on 100 rows finishes, API returns a valid answer Lesson: A project layout that grows into production Table: · devops · mlops · dataops DevOps MLOps DataOps What it ships Software Models and the pipelines that make them Data products and pipelines What changes behaviour Code Code, data and model Data and transformations Testing Unit and integration tests Plus data tests and model quality gates Data quality checks After release Uptime and errors Plus drift, accuracy and fairness Freshness and quality of data Typical tools Git, CI/CD, Docker, Kubernetes Plus MLflow, DVC, feature stores, registries Airflow, dbt, data contracts Lesson: DevOps, MLOps and DataOps compared Tips and traps In the exam The first block changes every time you run it, which is the point. With the seed fixed at 42, five runs give exactly the same accuracy, 0.972, five times. Over ten different seeds the accuracy has mean 0.956 and standard deviation 0.014, ranging from 0.937 to 0.972, so a difference of 0.01 between two models trained once means nothing.Lesson: Reproducibility: seeds and run records Remember The record says the wine data had 178 rows and 13 features with fingerprint 3a28849ef236, lists the parameters (C = 0.5, seed 7), the metrics (test accuracy 0.9778) and the Python, NumPy and scikit-learn versions. With this one JSON file, anyone can rebuild and check the model.Lesson: Reproducibility: seeds and run records In the exam The original breast cancer data gives a 5 fold accuracy of 0.981. Flipping 40 labels (7 percent) drops it to 0.882 with the same number of rows. Losing about 30 percent of the"],[10,"mlops-lifecycle","The MLOps lifecycle and reproducible projects","rows (399 left) gives 0.975. Each version has a different fingerprint, so the run record shows exactly which one a model saw.Lesson: Versioning data and models"],[10,"step-2","Step 2Build and ship","2.1"],[10,"mlops-pipelines","Pipelines and saving models","Open chapter Leak free scikit-learn pipelines, saving with joblib and metadata, file formats, and input contracts. Key ideas Pipeline Preprocessing plus model in one object. ColumnTransformer: numbers impute and scale, categories one hot. fit and transform Steps learn on training rows only, reuse at predict. handle_unknown='ignore' for unseen categories. Leakage No step may see test rows. Selection outside CV: 0.85 on noise, inside: 0.48. Split by time and group too. Saving joblib.dump the whole pipeline plus JSON metadata (features, classes, versions). Load trusted files only. Formats joblib for own Python services, skops for safe sharing, ONNX for other languages. Input contract Check presence, type, range, unknown categories, extra fields. Reject with 422. Formulas Standard scalingExam favourite z = (x − μtrain) / σtrain Statistics from the training data only. Lesson: One pipeline from raw columns to prediction Median imputationExam favourite missing x → median of training x Robust to outliers. Lesson: One pipeline from raw columns to prediction One hot columnsExam favourite one column per category seen in training Unknown category → all zeros with ignore. Lesson: One pipeline from raw columns to prediction Tables Table: kind of leakage · example · fix Kind of leakage Example Fix Preprocessing on all rows Scaler fitted before the split Scaler inside the pipeline Selection on all rows Top correlated columns chosen with test rows SelectKBest inside the pipeline Target leakage A column filled in after the outcome is known Remove it, check the time each column becomes available Time leakage Random split of time ordered data Split by time: train on the past, test on the future Group leakage The same patient in train and test GroupKFold by patient Lesson: Leakage: why every learning step belongs inside the pipeline Table: · joblib (pickle) · skops · onnx joblib (pickle) skops ONNX Framework format What it stores Any Python object scikit-learn objects A computation graph PyTorch state_dict, Keras .keras Safe to load from strangers No, can run code Yes, checks types first Yes Mostly (PyTorch weights_only loading) Needs Python to serve Yes, same versions Yes No: C++, C#, Java, JavaScript runtimes Usually Speed of serving Normal Normal Often faster with ONNX Runtime Normal Best for Your own models, quick start Sharing scikit-learn models Serving across languages and devices Deep learning models Lesson: Ways to save a model compared Tips and traps In the exam The churn pipeline reaches a 5 fold ROC AUC of 0.751 and a test accuracy of 0.69. After preprocessing, 5 raw columns have become 9 (3 scaled numbers and 6 one hot columns, with names such as cat__plan_basic). A new customer with a missing bill and a plan called student, which the model never saw, still gets a churn probability of 0.782 instead of an error.Lesson: One pipeline from raw columns to prediction In the exam On 100 rows of random noise with random labels, choosing the 20 best of 5000 columns before cross validation reports an accuracy of 0.85. Doing the same selection inside the pipeline reports 0.48, the honest answer for labels that are pure chance.Lesson: Leakage: why every learning step belongs inside the pipeline In the exam The fitted iris pipeline takes about 1.5 KB on disk. After loading, its predictions match the original on every row, and the flower [5.9, 3.0, 5.1, 1.8] is predicted as virginica. The JSON file records scikit-learn 1.8.0, which the serving side should match.Lesson: Saving and loading a trained pipeline Remember The first request passes. The second is rejected because tenure is −3 and the bill was sent as text. The third has four problems: a missing bill, 2.5 support calls where a whole number is required, an unknown plan, and an unexpected coupon field.Lesson: An input contract for the model 2.2"],[10,"mlops-serving","Serving models with Flask, FastAPI and Django","Open chapter Model APIs in Flask, FastAPI and Django, calling them on each OS, online against batch, and production servers sized with Little's law. Key ideas Model API Load once. /health plus POST /predict. JSON in and out. 200 success, 422 bad content, 405 wrong method, 500 is a bug. Frameworks Flask small. FastAPI checks Pydantic classes, docs at /docs. Django for a model inside a full web app. Test then call Test client in one process. Then curl (macOS, Linux, ChromeOS) or Invoke-RestMethod and curl.exe (Windows). Online or batch Online answers during the visit. Batch scores on a schedule, cheaper, less fresh. Read big files in chunks. Production servers Gunicorn on macOS and Linux, Waitress on Windows, Uvicorn for FastAPI everywhere. Several workers. Never app.run in production. Capacity L = λW. Workers = λ × latency / target use. M/M/1: W = 1/(μ − λ) explodes near capacity. Formulas LatencyExam favourite time from request sent to response received Report the median and the 95th or 99th percentile, not only the average. Lesson: A model behind a web API Throughput of one workerExam favourite requests per second = 1000 / latency in ms A worker that needs 20 ms serves at most 50 requests per second. Lesson: A model behind a web API Little’s lawExam favourite L = λ × W L requests in progress, λ arrivals per second, W seconds each request spends in the system. Lesson: From the development server to production Workers neededExam favourite workers = λ × latency / target use Aim for about 70 to 80 percent use so short bursts do not build a queue. Lesson: From the development server to production Waiting time for one worker (M/M/1 queue) W = 1 / (μ − λ) μ requests per second the worker can serve. W shoots up as λ nears μ. Lesson: From the development server to production Tables Table: status · meaning · when a model api sends it Status Meaning When a model API sends it 200 OK Success The prediction is in the body 400 Bad Request The request itself is broken The body is not valid JSON at all 405 Method Not Allowed Wrong method for this route GET sent to a POST only route 422 Unprocessable Content Valid JSON but the content fails the checks A word instead of a number, a missing field 500 Internal Server Error A bug or crash inside the service Never on purpose. Log it and fix it. Lesson: A model behind a web API Table: · flask · fastapi · django Flask FastAPI Django Model servers (BentoML, TorchServe, KServe) Size Small and simple Small, typed Large, everything included A platform Input checks Write them yourself Automatic from Pydantic classes Forms or Django REST framework serializers Built in per model API docs Add a package Automatic at /docs Add a package Usually automatic Production server Gunicorn, Waitress Uvicorn Gunicorn, Waitress Included Extras Many plugins Async, dependency injection Database, admin, users Batching requests, GPUs, scaling, model versions Best for Learning, small services New model APIs A model inside a larger web app Many models or heavy deep learning traffic Lesson: Flask, FastAPI, Django and model servers compared Tips and traps Remember The test client sends real requests to the app inside one Python process, so no port or network is needed. The good request gets 200 with virginica at 0.703. The broken one gets 422 and an error that tells the caller what to send. The model never sees bad data.Lesson: A model behind a web API In the exam One bad request produced three precise errors at once: sepal_length must be greater than 0, sepal_width is not a number, and petal_width is missing. The batch route scored two flowers in a single predict_proba call, virginica at 0.703 and setosa at 0.981.Lesson: FastAPI: the input contract written as a class Common mistake Django gives the same answers as Flask (200 with virginica at 0.703, 422 for bad JSON), and @require_POST turns a GET into 405 without running the view. In a real project the view lives in an app inside django-admin startproject, and the settings"],[10,"mlops-serving","Serving models with Flask, FastAPI and Django","come from environment variables, never a SECRET_KEY written in the code.Lesson: Django: when the model joins a full web application Remember 20,000 rows are scored in 4 chunks of 5,000. The last line times both styles on your own device, so the numbers change from run to run, but one call per row is always many times slower because each call pays the same fixed overhead.Lesson: Online against batch prediction In the exam A queue does not grow in a straight line. One worker that can serve 50 requests per second answers in 25 ms at 10 per second, 100 ms at 40 per second, and 1 full second at 49 per second. That is why services keep spare capacity.Lesson: From the development server to production 2.3"],[10,"mlops-docker","Docker and CI/CD for models","Open chapter A real Dockerfile and Compose file for a model API, per OS build steps, model behaviour tests and a GitHub Actions pipeline. Key ideas Image and container Image = OS layer, Python, packages, code, model. Container = running copy. Registry stores images. -p host:container. Dockerfile order Base, requirements, pip install, then code. Code change: 2.2 s instead of 32 s. Non root USER, HEALTHCHECK, CMD. Per system Docker Desktop on macOS and Windows (WSL 2), Engine plus docker group on Linux, Engine in the Linux environment on ChromeOS. Apple silicon: --platform linux/amd64. Model tests Quality gate, known examples, invariance, directional, latency. A constant model passes invariance, so use several kinds. CI/CD GitHub Actions: install, train, pytest, build, smoke test, push on main. First failure stops. Tag images by commit, secrets never in files. Numbers Flaky suite (1 − p)n. Accuracy SE √(p(1 − p)/n). Canary n = 4p(1 − p)/d2. Formulas Chance a suite of flaky tests is greenExam favourite P(green) = (1 − p)n n independent tests that each fail at random with probability p. Ten tests at 2 percent: 0.9810 = 0.817. Lesson: Testing a model, not only the code Uncertainty of a test accuracyExam favourite SE = √(p(1 − p) / n) With p = 0.9111 on n = 45 test rows, SE = 0.042, so a gate at 0.90 is less than one standard error away. Lesson: Testing a model, not only the code Tables Table: word · what it is · command Word What it is Command Image A read only package built from a Dockerfile, made of layers docker build -t iris-api:1.0.0 . Container A running copy of an image, with its own file system and network docker run -p 8000:8000 iris-api:1.0.0 Tag A name and version for an image iris-api:1.0.0 Registry A store for images that servers pull from docker push ghcr.io/owner/iris-api:1.0.0 Port mapping Connects a port on your computer to a port in the container -p 8000:8000 means computer 8000 to container 8000 Lesson: Containers, images and the Dockerfile Table: test · question it answers · in this project Test Question it answers In this project API test Do the routes answer correctly? test_health, test_known_flower Input test Is bad input rejected? test_bad_input_is_rejected (422) Quality gate Is the model good enough to ship? test_accuracy_gate (at least 0.90) Invariance Does tiny noise leave the answer alone? test_small_noise_keeps_the_class Directional Does a change push the answer the right way? test_longer_petals_mean_virginica Latency Is it fast enough? test_latency_budget (under 50 ms) Lesson: Testing a model, not only the code Table: · virtual environment · docker container · virtual machine Virtual environment Docker container Virtual machine Pins Python packages only Packages, Python, system libraries and OS files A whole operating system and kernel Start time Instant About a second Tens of seconds Size Hundreds of MB Hundreds of MB, shared layers Many GB Same on every computer No: OS and Python can differ Yes, for the same processor type Yes Isolation None Process level, shares the host kernel Strong, own kernel Best for Writing and testing code Shipping services, CI, Kubernetes Strict isolation or a different OS Lesson: Virtual environment, container or virtual machine Tips and traps In the exam Real timings of this project: with the good order, a one line change in app.py rebuilt in 2.2 seconds in total, because pip install came from the cache. With COPY . . before pip install, the same change took 32 seconds, because the 18 second install ran again.Lesson: Containers, images and the Dockerfile Remember The probability here is 0.653, not the 0.703 of MLOps 2.2, because train.py holds back 30 percent of the flowers for testing and trains on the rest. The image takes 555 MB on disk but only 129 MB is downloaded, because layers are stored compressed.Lesson: Build and run the model container on your computer Common mistake The broken model scored 0.2 and failed three of the four checks, so CI blocks it. Look closely:"],[10,"mlops-docker","Docker and CI/CD for models","it still passed the invariance test, because a model that ignores its input is perfectly stable. One kind of test is never enough.Lesson: Testing a model, not only the code"],[10,"step-3","Step 3Operate and improve","3.1"],[10,"mlops-tracking","Experiment tracking and the model registry","Open chapter Tracking runs, MLflow with per OS steps, the winner's curse, and a model registry with promotion and rollback. Key ideas Tracking Every run: parameters, metrics, artifacts, data fingerprint, Git commit, environment. Compare only runs on the same data. MLflow start_run, log_params, log_metrics, sklearn.log_model, search_runs. mlflow ui --backend-store-uri sqlite:///mlflow.db, port 5001 on macOS. Selection bias Best of many on one validation set flatters itself (0.615 vs 0.530). Untouched test set, report once. Gap about σ√(2 ln n). Registry Frozen numbered versions with lineage, movable aliases. Services load models:/name@champion. Promotion Same held out data, margin gate above noise, behaviour and fairness tests, human approval, logged reason. Rollback Move the alias back. No retraining, no rebuild. Formulas Random search coverageExam favourite P(at least one of n runs in the top q) = 1 − (1 − q)n 60 random runs reach the top 5 percent with 95 percent chance. Lesson: Choosing the best run without fooling yourself Expected best of n noisy scoresExam favourite about σ √(2 ln n) above the mean With σ = 0.02 and n = 100: 0.061, close to the 0.064 drop in the animation. Lesson: Choosing the best run without fooling yourself Tables Table: what to log · example · why What to log Example Why Parameters C = 1.0, class_weight = None To know what produced the result Metrics val_auc = 0.9952 To compare and rank runs Artifacts model file, confusion matrix plot To reuse the exact model later Data version a hash of the training data, or a DVC tag Scores on different data cannot be compared Code version Git commit id To rerun exactly the same code Environment Python and package versions The same code can behave differently with other versions Lesson: Experiment tracking: a record of every run Table: · mlflow · weights and biases · dvc MLflow Weights and Biases DVC TensorBoard Type Open source platform Hosted service (also self hosted) Open source, Git based Open source viewer Strength Tracking, registry and model packaging in one Rich dashboards and team features Versions data and pipelines next to Git Training curves for deep learning Model registry Yes Yes Through Git tags and its studio No Works offline Yes Mostly needs its server Yes Yes Best for A free, standard stack Teams who want polish and hosting Data versioning and reproducible pipelines Watching one training run Lesson: Tracking tools compared Tips and traps Remember Each line of runs.jsonl is one complete record: settings, score, a 12 character fingerprint of the training data and the Git commit. Because every run has the same data fingerprint, their scores are comparable. MLflow stores the same facts in a database instead of a text file.Lesson: Experiment tracking: a record of every run In the exam Four runs were logged and sorted by validation AUC. C = 1.0 won with 0.9952, was registered as version 1 of breast-cancer-classifier with the alias champion, and loading models:/breast-cancer-classifier@champion gave back exactly the same AUC.Lesson: MLflow on your own computer Common mistake The labels are coin flips, so every model is truly 50 percent accurate. The best of 1000 runs still showed 0.615 on validation, then 0.530 on fresh test data. With 200 validation rows one standard error is 0.035, and the best of 1000 lands about three standard errors above the truth.Lesson: Choosing the best run without fooling yourself In the exam v2 (AUC 0.8844) was kept but not promoted. v3 (0.9942) beat v1 (0.9555) and became champion. After the incident, champion moved back to v1 in one line, with no retraining and no rebuild.Lesson: The model registry: versions, aliases and promotion 3.2"],[10,"mlops-monitoring","Monitoring and drift","Open chapter What to monitor, PSI and KS drift tests, concept against data drift, retraining, and a scheduled drift job on every OS. Key ideas Four layers Service (p50, p95, p99, errors), data (missing, schema, drift), model (scores, accuracy), business. Percentiles, not means. SLO and budget Budget = (1 − SLO) × requests. Burn rate = actual / allowed error rate. Drift types Data (inputs move), label (class shares move), concept (rule changes, invisible to input checks). PSI and KS PSI = Σ(q − p) ln(q/p): 0.1 watch, 0.25 drift. KS = max CDF gap, very sensitive to sample size. Decay Concept drift: accuracy 0.95 to 0.57 with PSI 0.01. Data drift: PSI 6.2, accuracy 0.95. Retrain on schedule or trigger through the gate. Scheduling cron or launchd (macOS, Full Disk Access for Documents), schtasks (Windows), cron or systemd timer (Linux), GitHub Actions schedule anywhere. Formulas PercentileExam favourite p99 = the value that 99 percent of requests stay below Tail latency is what users notice. Lesson: What to watch once a model is live Error budgetExam favourite allowed failures = (1 − SLO) × requests 99.9 percent of 100,000 leaves a budget of 100 errors. Lesson: What to watch once a model is live Burn rate actual error rate / allowed error rate A burn rate of 5 uses a 30 day budget in 6 days. Lesson: What to watch once a model is live Population stability indexExam favourite PSI = Σbins (qi − pi) ln(qi / pi) p training shares, q live shares. Below 0.1 stable, 0.1 to 0.25 watch, above 0.25 drift. Lesson: Drift: when live data stops looking like training data Kolmogorov Smirnov statisticExam favourite D = maxx |Ftrain(x) − Flive(x)| Largest gap between the two cumulative curves. Lesson: Drift: when live data stops looking like training data PSI and KL divergence PSI = KL(q || p) + KL(p || q) For two normals with the same spread: (Δμ / σ)². Lesson: Drift: when live data stops looking like training data Tables Table: layer · examples · needs true labels? Layer Examples Needs true labels? Service p50, p95, p99 latency, error rate, requests per second No Data missing share, unseen categories, out of range values, PSI per feature No Model prediction mean and spread, PSI of the scores, accuracy, calibration Accuracy needs labels Business fraud losses, click through rate, churn Usually, and with delay Lesson: What to watch once a model is live Table: retraining strategy · when it fits · cost Retraining strategy When it fits Cost On a schedule (weekly, monthly) Steady, slow drift and labels arrive regularly Simple, may retrain without need On a trigger (accuracy or drift past a threshold) Irregular changes Needs reliable monitoring Online or continual learning Fast changing data such as ads or news Complex, risk of learning from bad data Lesson: Concept drift, label delay and retraining Table: · psi · ks test · chi square PSI KS test Chi square Wasserstein distance Data type Numeric (binned) or categories Numeric Categories Numeric Answers How big is the change Is there any change Is there any change How far did the values move Sensitive to sample size Little Very: big samples flag tiny changes Very Little Rule of thumb 0.1 watch, 0.25 drift p below 0.05, used with care p below 0.05, used with care Compare with the feature’s spread Lesson: When an alert fires Tips and traps Common mistake The average latency is 24.5 ms, which sounds fine, but the 99th percentile is 292 ms, so 1 request in 100 is ten times slower than typical. Only 98.27 percent of requests met the 200 ms target, below the 99 percent SLO, while errors used 65 percent of the day’s budget.Lesson: What to watch once a model is live In the exam A shift of 8 in the mean gives PSI 0.610, clear drift. A wider spread gives 0.246 with no change in the mean, which a check on averages would miss. With 200,000 rows a harmless shift of 0.3 already has a KS p value of 1.3e−12, while PSI is 0.001. Use the size of the change to decide, and the p value only as a hint.Lesson: Drift: when live"],[10,"mlops-monitoring","Monitoring and drift","data stops looking like training data In the exam Under concept drift accuracy fell from 0.95 to 0.57 in a year while the PSI of the input stayed at 0.01. Retraining every 3 months kept it between 0.86 and 0.94. Under data drift the PSI reached 6.20 but accuracy stayed at 0.94 to 0.95, because the model’s rule still held.Lesson: Concept drift, label delay and retraining Remember On the demo data the income column moved (PSI 0.472) and 6 percent of ages went missing, above the 5 percent limit, so both are flagged. The score column needs watching (0.155). With --strict the job exits with code 1, which schedulers and CI treat as a failure.Lesson: Run a drift check every day on your computer 3.3"],[10,"mlops-explain-fair","Explainability and fairness","Open chapter Permutation importance, Shapley values and SHAP, fairness metrics by group, mitigation, impossibility results and model cards. Key ideas Global importance Permutation on test data: I = s − mean shuffled score. Impurity importance is computed on training data and favours many valued features. Shapley values φ averages what a feature adds over every order. Base + Σφ = prediction. 2|F| coalitions, so use TreeExplainer or sampling. Explanations Show how the model used a feature, not a cause. They depend on the background data. Counterfactuals give actionable changes. Group metrics Disparate impact = rate B / rate A, flag below 0.8. Equal opportunity: equal TPR. Equalized odds: equal TPR and FPR. Mitigation Pre processing (data), in processing (constrained loss), post processing (group thresholds). Re-measure everything afterwards. Impossibility and documentation Different base rates: calibration and equal error rates conflict. Pick a rule for the harm, write a model card, gate promotion on it. Formulas Permutation importanceExam favourite Ij = s − (1/K) Σk sk,j s is the test score, sk,j the score after the k-th shuffle of feature j. Lesson: Which features does the model use? Impurity importance Ij = Σsplits on j (nnode/n) Δimpurity Measured on training data, biased towards features with many values. Lesson: Which features does the model use? Shapley valueExam favourite φj = ΣS ⊆ F ∖ j [ |S|! (|F| − |S| − 1)! / |F|! ] · [ v(S ∪ j) − v(S) ] v(S) is the average prediction when only the features in S are known. Lesson: Shapley values: why this one prediction? EfficiencyExam favourite f(x) = φ0 + Σj φj φ0 is the base value, the average prediction. Lesson: Shapley values: why this one prediction? Cost of the exact sum 2|F| coalitions 3 features need 8, 20 features already need 1,048,576. Lesson: Shapley values: why this one prediction? Disparate impact ratioExam favourite DI = P(ŷ = 1 | B) / P(ŷ = 1 | A) The four fifths rule flags DI below 0.8. Lesson: Is the model fair to every group? Equal opportunity gapExam favourite TPRA − TPRB, with TPR = P(ŷ = 1 | y = 1) Zero means qualified people are treated alike. Lesson: Is the model fair to every group? Equalized odds TPRA = TPRB and FPRA = FPRB Both kinds of error are shared equally. Lesson: Is the model fair to every group? Chouldechova’s identityExam favourite FPR = [p / (1 − p)] · [(1 − PPV) / PPV] · (1 − FNR) p is the base rate. With equal PPV and FNR, different p forces different FPR. Lesson: Fixing unfairness, and why you cannot have every kind Tables Table: method · data it uses · strength · weakness Method Data it uses Strength Weakness Impurity (tree models) Training Free, instant Favours many valued features, says nothing about test data Permutation Test Any model, measures real dependence Correlated features share or hide credit Mean |SHAP| Any Consistent with local explanations Slower for non tree models Lesson: Which features does the model use? Table: · demographic parity · equal opportunity · equalized odds Demographic parity Equal opportunity Equalized odds Calibration by group Asks for equal Approval rate TPR TPR and FPR Meaning of a score Uses true labels No Yes Yes Yes Fits when Labels themselves are biased Missing a qualified person is the main harm Both errors harm people Scores are read as probabilities Main cost Can approve unqualified people FPR may differ Usually lowers accuracy most Rates may differ between groups Lesson: Fixing unfairness, and why you cannot have every kind Table: model card section · example for the loan model Model card section Example for the loan model Intended use Rank loan applications for human review. Not for automatic refusal. Training data 20,000 past applications, 2019 to 2024, one country Metrics Accuracy 0.637 overall, reported per group Fairness Equal opportunity gap 0.00 after group thresholds, disparate impact 0.92 Explanations SHAP top three reasons sent with every refusal Limits Not tested on self employed"],[10,"mlops-explain-fair","Explainability and fairness","applicants. Retrain if PSI of income passes 0.25 (MLOps 3.2). Lesson: Model cards and responsible release Tips and traps In the exam Impurity importance always adds up to 1 and gives worst radius 0.442. On test data, shuffling worst radius costs 0.170 accuracy, the real dependence. The random ID column, pure noise, still got 0.018 of the impurity credit because its many distinct values offer many split points on training data. Shuffling it changed the test score by −0.008, which is just noise.Lesson: Which features does the model use? In the exam The base value is 0.450, the average predicted chance of repaying. This applicant’s debt ratio of 0.82 pulls the prediction down by 0.194, the low income by 0.121 and the young age by 0.052, ending at 0.083. The three contributions add up exactly from 0.450 to 0.083, which is the efficiency property.Lesson: Shapley values: why this one prediction? In the exam With one threshold of 0.5 group B is approved 33 percent of the time against 64 percent for group A, a disparate impact of 0.52, far below the four fifths rule of 0.8. Among people who did repay, B was approved 48 percent of the time and A 75 percent. Lowering B’s threshold to 0.38 closed the TPR gap, raised the ratio to 0.92 and cost only 0.010 accuracy (0.647 to 0.637).Lesson: Is the model fair to every group? Remember TreeExplainer works in log odds for this model, and for the first applicant 0.027 + 0.489 + 0.027 + 0.401 = 0.943, exactly the model’s own log odds. Postcode gets a small mean |SHAP| of 0.055, yet the audit still shows a TPR gap of 0.196 between groups, because income also differs between groups. ThresholdOptimizer cut the gap to 0.018 while overall accuracy went from 0.672 to 0.665.Lesson: SHAP and Fairlearn on your own computer Remember Explainability and fairness checks belong in the same CI pipeline as accuracy tests (MLOps 2.3). A gate such as “fail if disparate impact is below 0.8 or the TPR gap is above 0.05” stops an unfair model from being promoted, in the same way as a failing unit test.Lesson: Model cards and responsible release"],[10,"step-4","Step 4Design and certify","4.1"],[10,"mlops-system-design","Designing an ML system end to end","Open chapter Sizing with QPS and Little's law, serving modes, skew, shadow and canary releases, A/B test sample size and peeking, and a local load test. Key ideas Design framework Goal and metric, requirements as numbers, data and features, baseline model, serving, offline then online evaluation, monitoring and retraining. Sizing QPS = daily requests / 86,400, times a peak factor. Little’s law: throughput = workers / latency. Replicas = ceil(peak / (capacity × load)). Serving modes Batch (scheduled, stale), online (ms, tail latency), streaming (fresh features). Retrieve then rank for big catalogues. Skew Same feature, different code in training and serving. One definition or feature store, logged features, parity test. Safe release Shadow, canary 1 to 100 percent with guardrails, automatic rollback, keep the old version ready. A/B tests n = (za + zb)² 2p̄(1 − p̄) / δ². Fix n first, test once. Peeking: 4.4 to 22 percent false alarms. Formulas Average loadExam favourite QPS = daily requests / 86,400 A day has 86,400 seconds. Lesson: Start with the problem, then size the system Replicas replicas = ⌈ peak QPS / (per replica QPS × target load) ⌉ A target load of 0.6 leaves room for spikes and a failed replica. Lesson: Start with the problem, then size the system Sample size per groupExam favourite n = (z1−α/2 + z1−β)² · 2 p̄(1 − p̄) / δ² p̄ is the average rate, δ the lift. Halving δ needs four times the users. Lesson: A/B testing a new model Two proportion z testExam favourite z = (pB − pA) / √( p(1 − p)(1/nA + 1/nB) ) p is the pooled rate of both groups. Lesson: A/B testing a new model Many looks P(at least one false alarm) ≤ 1 − (1 − α)k An upper limit for k looks. Real looks share data, so the true rate is lower but still far above α. Lesson: A/B testing a new model Tables Table: · batch · online (on request) · streaming Batch Online (on request) Streaming When it predicts On a schedule, for everyone When a request arrives When an event arrives Latency Hours, results read from a table Milliseconds Seconds Feature freshness As old as the last run Request data plus stored features Features updated from the event stream Example Nightly churn scores for the sales team Fraud check during a payment Live delivery time estimates Main risk Stale scores, wasted work for inactive users Tail latency and cost Complex pipelines (Kafka, Flink) Lesson: Batch, online and streaming predictions Table: strategy · users see the new model · good for · watch out for Strategy Users see the new model Good for Watch out for Shadow No Latency, crashes, agreement with the old model Cannot measure user reactions, doubles compute Canary A small growing share Catching bugs with little harm Small shares need time to show rare problems Blue-green All at once, with an instant switch back Simple, fast rollback No gradual exposure A/B test A fixed random half (or share) Measuring the effect on a business metric Needs enough users and a fixed plan Multi-armed bandit More traffic to the version that is winning Many variants, short campaigns Harder to read the final effect Lesson: Shadow, canary and rollback Table: part of the design · choice · why · chapter Part of the design Choice Why Chapter Metric Fraud losses saved, with a cap on wrongly blocked good payments Accuracy is useless at 0.1 percent fraud ML 2.x metrics Features Payment amount, merchant, card history counts over 1 hour and 30 days History counts come from a feature store, updated from the payment stream This chapter Model Gradient boosted trees, a few milliseconds per payment Strong on tabular data, fast, explainable with SHAP MLOps 3.3 Serving Online, inside the payment flow, rules as a fallback Must decide before the payment completes MLOps 2.2 Rollout Shadow for two weeks, then canary with guardrails on block rate Fraud labels are slow, so start with safe signals This chapter Monitoring Score PSI, block rate, latency p99, dispute rate when labels arrive Fraudsters adapt, so concept drift is"],[10,"mlops-system-design","Designing an ML system end to end","certain MLOps 3.2 Retraining Weekly through the tested pipeline and the registry gate New fraud patterns appear all the time MLOps 3.1 Lesson: Worked design: real time fraud detection Tips and traps In the exam Ten million daily users with 20 requests each average 2,315 requests per second and peak at 6,944. One replica with 4 workers at 40 ms handles 100 per second, so 116 replicas keep each at 60 percent load. Ranking a million items per request would take a full second, which is why recommenders retrieve 500 candidates first.Lesson: Start with the problem, then size the system Common mistake The model scored 0.730 offline. The serving code filled missing spend with 0 instead of the training median and received spend in pence, not pounds, so live accuracy fell to 0.550 and the mean predicted churn collapsed from 0.435 to 0.124. A parity test on the same raw rows finds the difference on 100 percent of rows before release.Lesson: Batch, online and streaming predictions In the exam To detect a lift from 5.0 to 5.5 percent with α = 0.05 and 80 percent power you need 31,235 users per group. In 2,000 A/A tests a single look at the end gave 4.4 percent false alarms, close to the promised 5. Stopping on the first day with p < 0.05 gave 22.0 percent, more than four times as many.Lesson: A/B testing a new model In the exam The built-in demo server can work on 4 requests at once, each taking 20 ms, so Little’s law predicts 4 / 0.020 = 200 requests per second. The test gets close to 200 with 4 users and stays just under 200 with 8 and 16 users, while the median latency rises from about 21 ms to about 40 ms and then higher. Past the saturation point, more users only add waiting time. Timings change a little on every run.Lesson: Load test your model service on your own computer Remember The real FastAPI container from MLOps 2.3 levelled off at about 1,180 requests per second from 8 users, while its p50 went from 2.1 ms to 12.1 ms at 16 users. For the 6,944 requests per second peak, that machine would need about 10 such replicas at 60 percent load, a number you can now defend.Lesson: Load test your model service on your own computer Remember Interviewers look for the trade-offs, not one right answer. Say what you would measure, what you would try first, and how you would know it failed.Lesson: Worked design: real time fraud detection 4.2"],[10,"mlops-revision","MLOps revision and certification practice","Open chapter The whole subject as one loop, maturity levels, a release gate across all chapters, a capstone, cloud service mapping and exam practice. Key ideas The loop Setup, lifecycle, pipelines, serving, Docker and CI/CD, tracking, monitoring, explain and fair, system design, then retrain. Maturity Level 0 manual, level 1 continuous training through a pipeline, level 2 CI/CD for the pipeline itself. Release gate Parity, quality gain on the same holdout, fairness (DI ≥ 0.8, TPR gap), score PSI, size. Any FAIL blocks promotion. Key numbers PSI 0.1 and 0.25. DI 0.8. Burn rate 14.4 for 2 percent in an hour. Peeking 4.4 to 22 percent. Layer cache 2.2 s against 32 s. Capstone Fingerprint, pipeline, tracked run, alias, tested API (200, 422), drift check, model card. Cloud mapping Pipelines, endpoints, tracking, registry, monitoring, explainability and feature store exist on Vertex AI, SageMaker and Azure ML under their own names. Formulas PSIExam favourite PSI = Σ (q − p) ln(q / p) 0.1 watch, 0.25 drift (3.2). Lesson: One release gate that uses every chapter Little’s lawExam favourite workers = request rate × latency Sizing workers and replicas (2.2, 4.1). Lesson: One release gate that uses every chapter Disparate impactExam favourite DI = rateB / rateA ≥ 0.8 Four fifths rule (3.3). Lesson: One release gate that uses every chapter Error budget and burn rateExam favourite budget = (1 − SLO) × requests, burn = actual / allowed A burn rate of 14.4 spends 2 percent of a 30 day budget in an hour (3.2). Lesson: One release gate that uses every chapter Shapley efficiency f(x) = φ0 + Σ φj Contributions add up to the prediction (3.3). Lesson: One release gate that uses every chapter A/B sample size n = (za + zb)² 2p̄(1 − p̄) / δ² Fix n before the test (4.1). Lesson: One release gate that uses every chapter Tables Table: chapter · key idea · classic trap Chapter Key idea Classic trap 1.1 Setup A venv per project, exact versions in requirements.txt Installing into the system Python, committing .venv 1.2 Lifecycle Seeds, run records, data fingerprints Reporting the best seed instead of the spread 2.1 Pipelines Fit on training rows only, save one pipeline Scaling or selecting features before the split 2.2 Serving Validate input, 422 for bad requests Loading the model inside the request handler 2.3 Docker, CI/CD Requirements before code, gate every release Running as root, testing only the code and not the model 3.1 Tracking Log every run, aliases for promotion Choosing the best of many runs on the validation set and reporting that score 3.2 Monitoring PSI for size, labels for accuracy Trusting KS p values on huge samples, missing concept drift 3.3 Explain, fair Permutation importance on test data, rates by group Thinking that dropping the group column removes bias 4.1 System design Numbers first, safe rollout, honest A/B test Stopping a test the first day p < 0.05 Lesson: The whole subject in one loop Table: level · what is automated · what starts a new model · tools from this subject Level What is automated What starts a new model Tools from this subject 0 Manual Only serving A person, often months apart Notebooks, a saved model file (2.1) 1 Pipeline automation Data checks, training, validation, delivery Schedule, new data or a drift alert Pipelines (2.1), tracking and registry (3.1), monitoring (3.2) 2 CI/CD automation Also testing, building and deploying the pipeline Also every code change Docker and GitHub Actions (2.3), registry gates (3.1) Lesson: MLOps maturity levels Table: idea (chapter) · open source here · google cloud · aws Idea (chapter) Open source here Google Cloud AWS Azure Pipelines (2.1, 2.3) scikit-learn Pipeline, GitHub Actions Vertex AI Pipelines SageMaker Pipelines Azure ML pipelines Serving (2.2) FastAPI, Docker Vertex AI endpoints SageMaker endpoints Azure ML online endpoints Tracking (3.1) MLflow Vertex AI Experiments SageMaker with managed MLflow Azure ML with MLflow Registry (3.1) MLflow Model Registry Vertex AI"],[10,"mlops-revision","MLOps revision and certification practice","Model Registry SageMaker Model Registry Azure ML model registry Monitoring (3.2) PSI job, cron Vertex AI Model Monitoring SageMaker Model Monitor Azure ML model monitoring Explain and fairness (3.3) SHAP, Fairlearn Vertex Explainable AI SageMaker Clarify Responsible AI dashboard Feature store (4.1) one shared feature function Vertex AI Feature Store SageMaker Feature Store Azure ML managed feature store Lesson: The same ideas on cloud platforms Tips and traps Remember Level 2 is not always the goal. A small team with one model retrained monthly may be well served by level 1. Match the automation to how often the data and the code change.Lesson: MLOps maturity levels In the exam Challenger 1 has the higher AUC, 0.0498 above the champion, because it uses postcode and the past labels favoured group A. The gate blocks it: the equal opportunity gap is 0.227 and the disparate impact is 0.48. Challenger 2 drops postcode, still gains 0.0125 AUC, passes all six checks and is promoted. A higher score on biased labels is not a better model.Lesson: One release gate that uses every chapter Remember The test AUC is 0.7038 on synthetic data. The API returns 200 for a good request, 422 for a negative tenure and still 200 for the unseen plan family, because the encoder ignores unknown categories. After a 20 percent price rise the PSI of monthly_bill is 0.334, so the drift check says drift. The model card records all of it.Lesson: Capstone: the whole lifecycle on your computer In the exam A common exam pattern: a scenario with stale models and manual retraining, and four services to choose from. The answer is the pipeline plus monitoring service of that platform, because the problem is level 0 maturity, not the model.Lesson: The same ideas on cloud platforms"]]};
